1 NOTE KING OF NORWAY
1 NOTE Källa:http://harmkraka.se/slaktforskning/fm/index.htm
1 NOTE Haakon regnes som den siste "norske" konge. Han bygde Akershus slott og
2 CONT flyttet hovedstaden fra Bergen til Oslo.
1 NOTE     * RESIDENCE: Den siste "norske" konge
2 CONT     * OCCUPATION: Konge 1299-1319
2 CONT     * BIRTH: 10 Apr 1270
2 CONT     * DEATH: 8 May 1319, Tunsberghus
2 CONT     * BURIAL: 1319, Mariakirken, Oslo 
1 NOTE King of Norway 1299-1319.
2 CONT The 'last' Norwegian king.
2 CONT 6716778. Kong Håkon V MAGNUSSON av Norge(3480) was born in 1270 in Tunsberghus.(4) (3481) He was a Hertugnavn in 1273. (3482) Ved farens død 1280, hertug over oplandene, Oslo og Færøene. He was a Konge in 1299 in Norges.(3483) Ve
2 CONC d broren Eriks død. He died on 8 May 1319 in Tunsberghus. (3484) Han ble gravlagt i Mariakirken i Oslo og var den siste av Sverres ætt. Han regjerte med kraft, opphevet jarle- og lendermannstitlene og begrenset biskeppenes verdsli
2 CONC ge makt, fortsatte krigen med D. til 1309, da fred ble sluttet i Kjøbenhavn.
1 NOTE Utenfor ekteskap: Agnes Haakonsdtr. Han ble gift med Eufemi a von Rügen
2 CONT (1275-1.5.1312 i Norge), fikk med henne dattere n Ingeborg (1301-1360).
1 NOTE KING OF NORWAY
1 NOTE Haakon ble Hertug i 1277, og da faren døde var han Hertug over Oplandene, Oslo og Færøyene.Da broren døde i 1299 ble Haakon Norges konge. Han regjerte med kraft og opphevde jarle og lendermannstitlene, og begrenset kirkens verdsli
2 CONC ge makt. Konge i Norge mellom 1299 - 1319.
1 NOTE SOURCE NOTES:
2 CONT http://www.haaversen.com/geneology/fowsrc.html
2 CONT EB(1980) v. IV, p. 821.
2 CONT http://home.sol.no/~nermo/slekt/ ...
1 NOTE RESEARCH NOTES:
2 CONT "Longlegs"; King of Norway 1299-1319; Abolished title of baron 1308. Built
2 CONT Akershus.  sons Jon Sudreim & Sigurd Sudreim.
2 CONT   The younger son of Magnus VI Lawmender, Haakon succeeded his older brother,
2 CONT Erik II Magnusson, in 1299. Determined to reduce the power of the higher
2 CONT nobles and clerics, which had increased during Erik's reign, Haakon abolished
2 CONT the title of baron in 1308, removed members of the aristocracy from the royal
2 CONT council, and regained the right to appoint selected priests. He built several
2 CONT royal fortresses, most notably the one at Akershus (in use until 1815). His
2 CONT shift of the centre of the government eastward from the North Sea ports
2 CONT reflected the decline in contacts with Norway's western colonies and in royal
2 CONT revenues from trade.
2 CONT
2 CONT Haakon continued the preferential treatment given to Hanseatic traders by his
2 CONT brother Erik (reigned 1280-99), thus arousing the resentment of English
2 CONT merchants. He complemented his anti-English trade policy by siding with the
2 CONT Scots in their revolt against English rule. He also was embroiled in
2 CONT intermittent wars against Denmark and Sweden throughout his reign.
2 CONT
2 CONT Haakon and especially his wife, Euphemia, were enthusiastic patrons of the
2 CONT arts and commissioned the writing and translation of many chivalric romances.
2 CONT Because he had no sons, he revised the law of succession, enabling Magnus VII
2 CONT Eriksson, the son of his daughter Ingeborg and her husband, the Swedish prince
2 CONT Erik, to succeed him.
