1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT
2 CONT =Tostiq Godwinson=
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tostig_Godwinson From WIkipedia:]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Tostig Godwinson''' (1026? – September 25, 1066) was an Anglo-Saxon earl of Northumbria and brother of King Harold II of England, the last crowned Anglo-Saxon King of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Early life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Tostig was the third child of Godwin, Earl of Wessex and Kent, and Gytha Thorkelsdóttir. In 1051, he married Judith, the daughter of Count Baldwin IV, half-sister of Baldwin V of Flanders, and aunt of Matilda who married William t
2 CONC he Conqueror. This made him William's uncle-in-law.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Earldom'''
2 CONT
2 CONT That same year, 1051, Tostig and his father were banished from England to which they forcefully returned in 1052. Three years later in 1055, Tostig became the Earl of Northumbria upon the death of Earl Siward.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tostig appears to have governed in Northumbria with some difficulty. He was never popular with the Northumbrian ruling class, a mix of Danish invaders,and Anglo Saxon survivors of the last Norse invasion. The reasons for this are 
2 CONC not clear. Tostig was known to have taken a heavy hand against those who resisted his rule, including the murder of several scions of Northumbrian families. The reasons for this resistance include frequent absences at the court of
2 CONC  King Edward in the south, and possibly a lack of leadership against the Scots, voracious raiders, whose king was a personal friend of Tostig. This was a Catch-22 situation, however; Tostig's unpopularity made it difficult to rais
2 CONC e local levies to combat the Scots. He resorted to using a strong force of Danish mercenaries (housecarles) as his main force, an expensive and resented policy (the housecarle's leaders were later slaughtered by rebels). Local bia
2 CONC ses probably also played a part. Tostig was of the south of England, a distinctly different culture from the north, which had not bent its head to a southern earl in many lifetimes. In 1063, still immersed in the confused local po
2 CONC litics of Northumbria, his popularity apparently plummeted to a new and dangerous level. Many of the inhabitants of Northumbria were Danes, who had enjoyed lesser taxation than in other parts of England. Yet the wars in Wales, of 
2 CONC which Tostig's constituents were principal beneficiaries, needed paying for. Tostig had been a major commander in these wars attacking in the north whilst his brother Harold marched up from the south.
2 CONT
2 CONT In late 1063 or early 1064, Tostig had Gamal, son of Orm and Ulf, son of Dolfin, assassinated when they visited him under safe conduct. Also, the Vita Edwardi, otherwise sympathetic to Tostig, states that he had 'repressed [the No
2 CONC rthumbrians] with the heavy yoke of his rule'. This may refer to any or all of the situations discussed above.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 3 October 1065, the thegns of Yorkshire and the rest of Yorkshire descended on York and occupied the city. They killed Tostig's officials and supporters, then declared Tostig outlawed for his unlawful action and sent for Morcar
2 CONC , younger brother of Edwin, Earl of Mercia. The northern rebels marched south to press their case with King Edward. They were joined at Northampton by Earl Edwin and his forces. There, they were met by Earl Harold, who had been se
2 CONC nt by King Edward to negotiate with them and thus did not bring his forces. After Harold had spoken with the rebels at Northampton, he realised that Tostig would not be able to retain Northumbria. When he returned to Oxford, where
2 CONC  the royal council was to meet on 28 October, he had probably already made up his mind.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Exile and rebellion'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Harold persuaded the king to agree to the demands of the rebels. Tostig was outlawed a short time later, possibly early in November, because he refused to accept his deposition as commanded by Edward. This led to the fatal confron
2 CONC tation and enmity between the Godwinsons. At a meeting of the king and his council, Tostig publicly accused Harold of fomenting the rebellion, truly an outrageous claim to make, unless there was a grain of truth. Harold certainly 
2 CONC rid himself of a troublesome and ambitious brother, one who may have been angling for the throne. Also, Harold was keen to unify England in the face of the grave threat from William of Normandy, who had openly declared his intenti
2 CONC on to take the English throne. Perhaps Harold sold out his unpopular brother to ensure peace and support. Tostig certainly thought so.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tostig took ship with his family and some loyal thegns and took refuge with his father-in-law, Count Baldwin IV. He even attempted to form an alliance with William. Baldwin provided him with a fleet and he landed in the Isle of Wi
2 CONC ght in May 1066, where he collected money and provisions, and he raided the coast as far as Sandwich. King Harold called out land and naval forces and Tostig retreated. He moved north and after an unsuccessful attempt to get his b
2 CONC rother Gyrth to join him, he raided Norfolk and Lincolnshire. The Earls Edwin and Morcar defeated him decisively, and deserted by his men, he fled to his sworn brother, King Malcolm III of Scotland. Tostig spent the summer of 1066
2 CONC  in Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT He made contact in some way with King Harald III Hardrada of Norway and persuaded him to invade England. One of the sagas claims that he sailed for Norway, and greatly impressed the Norwegian king and his court, managing to sway a
2 CONC  decidedly unenthusiastic Harald, who had just concluded a long and inconclusive war with Denmark, into raising a levy to take the throne of England. With Hardrada's aid, Tostig sailed up the Humber and defeated Morcar and Edwin a
2 CONC t Gate Fulford.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Battle of Stamford Bridge'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Hardrada's army invaded York, taking hostages after a peaceful surrender, and likely agreed with the local inhabitants to gather commandeered supplies at Stamford Bridge, near York, a conveniently central spot, well fed by streams
2 CONC  and roads. King Harold Godwinson raced northward with an English army from London and, on September 25, 1066, surprised Tostig and about 6,000 of his men, basking in the sun and awaiting supplies. The Norwegians, and the Flemish 
2 CONC mercenaries hired by Tostig, were largely without armor and carried only personal weapons. The day was very hot and no resistance was expected. The remainder of the 11,000 man force remained guarding the Norse ships, beached miles
2 CONC  away at Riccall. After a brief meeting of the two kings, where Harald refused to surrender, and Tostig to abandon him, a long battle ensued. Despite making a brave stand, and reinforced late in the day by a desperate, sweating co
2 CONC lumn from Riccall, the Norwegians suffered a complete and utter defeat. Fewer than twenty of the three hundred Norwegian ships returned home. King Harald of Norway died there, as did Tostig Godwinson.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his death, Tostig's two sons took refuge in Norway, while his wife Judith married Duke Welf of Bavaria. It is believed that after Stamford Bridge his body was taken to York and buried at York Minster.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''His two sons with Judith:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Skuli Tostisson Kongsfostre (born 1052)-his great-great-granddaugther Helena Guttormsdotter was the mistress of Valdemar II of Denmark and mother of Vlademar son Canute, Duke of Reval.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Ketil Tostisson (born 1054)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl of Northumbria from 1055 until his exile in 1065 following a revolt
2 CONT
2 CONT against his misrule. He allied with Harold III Hardraade of Norway and
2 CONT
2 CONT invaded N England but was killed by his brother Harold at the Battle of Stamford Bridge (River Derwent).
2 CONT
2 CONT Reference:  http://www.lillebye.no/ane12.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl of Northampton and Nottingham.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Universal Identifier from University of Hull Royal Database England:
2 CONT
2 CONT 0CFDCBED0D75452BBC595D3B767B701DDE78)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Death'''
2 CONT
2 CONT On September 25, 1066, King Harold II of England (Tostig's brother) marched his army from the south of England where they were awaiting the Normans up to York and halted the Norwegian invasion at the Battle of Stamford Bridge, in 
2 CONC which Tostig and Harald III were both killed. After the death of Tostig, his two sons took refuge in Norway, while his wife Judith married Duke Welf of Bavaria. It is believed that after Stamford Bridge his body was taken to York 
2 CONC and buried at York Minster.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Trivia'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In Julian Rathbone's historical novel, "The Last English King", Tostig is depicted as Edward the Confessor's catamite.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Notes'''
2 CONT
