2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Was the Grand Prince of Kiev for only one week.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note on titles: "Bеликий Kнязь" sometimes is translated as "Grand Prince", other times as "Grand Duke", and other times as "King". The first Grand Prince to formally use the title Tsar was Ivan IV (Ivan the Terrible, 1530-1584), t
2 CONC he last of the Rurik dinasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikipedia:
2 CONT
2 CONT Rostislav Mstislavich (Russian: Ростислав Мстиславич) (c.1110-1167), Kniaz' (Prince) of Smolensk (1125-1160), Novgorod (1153) and Velikiy Kniaz (Grand Prince) of Kiev (1154, 1159-1167). He was the son of Mstislav I of Kiev.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Yaroslav II of Kiev was driven out of Novgorod, Rostislav was invited to become the ruler of Novgorod. He accepted, and became the prince on April 17, 1154. Then, learning that Iziaslav II had died, Rostislav left Novgorod t
2 CONC o take the Kievan throne. Indignant that their prince had abandoned them and angered that "he did not make order among them, but tore them more apart", the citizens of Novgorod drove out Rostislav's son, David, who was their gover
2 CONC nor. They replaced him with Mstislav, the son of Yury Dolgoruky.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rostislav ruled Kiev for one week before Iziaslav III of Kiev forced him to flee to Chernigov.
2 CONT --------------------
