2 CONT
2 CONT Harald III Sigurdsson Hardråde, 1015-1066, sønn av kong Sigurd Syr på Ringerike og Åsta Gudbrandsdatter.  Deltok i slaget på Stiklestad på sin halvbror kong Olavs side. Flyktet deretter til Gardarike.  Fra 1034 kom han i den gresk
2 CONC e keisers tjeneste som høvding blant «væringene» i Miklagard.  Ble gift med storfyrst Jaroslavs datter Ellisiv i Gardarike.  I 1045 vendte Harald tilbake til Norge med en stor formue og fikk halvdelen av riket mot å skifte sitt gu
2 CONC ll med kong Magnus.  Ved Magnus' død 1047 ble Harald enekonge i Norge og Danmark. 1062 gjorde Harald et stort hærtog mot den danske konge Svend,  og slo ham ved Nissan i Halland. Men striden fortsatte, og i 1064 anerkjente Harald 
2 CONC Svends kongedømme i Danmark.  Først med Harald Haråde kan man si at rikssamlingen var fullbyrdet. Han styrte med stor hardhet og kom i strid med flere stormenn,  bl.a. til Einar Tambarskjelve, som han lot drepe sammen med. sønnen 
2 CONC Eindride. Harald begynte å bygge Mariakirkene i Oslo og Nidaros,  begge i stein. Etter tradisjonen anla han Oslo. Harald drev en kraftig Vesterhavspolitikk, og Orknøyene og Hjaltland ble fast knyttet til Norge.  I 1066 drog Harald
2 CONC  på hærtog til England for å støtte den engelske tronkrever, jarlen Toste Godwinsson.  Han møtte den engelske kongen Harald Godwinsson i slaget ved Stamford Bridge, Harald falt.  Harald etterlot to sønner med sin frille Tora Torbe
2 CONC rgsdotter, Magnus og Olav.  Med Harald Hardrådes død regnes slutten på vikingetiden.
2 CONT Magnus II Haraldsson, 1048-69, sønn av Harald Hardråde, konge fra 1066 over den nordlige del av Norge, i det han delte riket med broren, Olav Kyrre.
2 CONT Olav III Haraldsson Kyrre, ca. 1050-93, sønn av Harald Hardråde, ble konge 1066 sammen med. broren Magnus, enekonge fra 1069.  Tilnavnet Kyrre betyr «den fredelige». Olav regnes som grunnleggeren av Bergen. I hans regjeringstid op
2 CONC plevde landet rik økonomisk fremgang.
1 NOTE the king of Norway from 1047 until 1066.
2 CONT
2 CONT Harald was the youngest of King Olaf II's three half-brothers born to Åsta Gudbrandsdatter. When Harald was 15, King Olaf was killed defending his throne from Canute the Great in 1030 at the Battle of Stiklestad. Harald took part 
2 CONC in the battle and although wounded managed to escape before leaving Norway in exile. He was able to form a band of warriors out of men who had also been exiled as a result of Olaf's death. In 1031 Harald and his men reached the la
2 CONC nd of the Kievan Rus where they served the armies of Yaroslav I the Wise, the King of the Rus. Harald is thought to have taken part in King Yaroslav's campaign against the Poles and was appointed joint commander of defense forces.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some years after Harald and his men had entered the land of the Rus, they packed up and left for the heart of the Byzantine Empire, the city of Constantinople. At the time, the Byzantine Empire was the wealthiest empire in Medieva
2 CONC l Europe and the Near East. Harald and his men pledged themselves to the service of the armies of the empire. Harald's forces joined the elite mercenary unit known as the Varangian Guard. It was not long until Harald had proven hi
2 CONC mself in battle and gained the respect of his fellow guardsmen. Harald became the leader of the entire force and used this power to undertake his own missions. Harald's forces won a great many victories in North Africa, Syria and 
2 CONC Sicily. Through ingenuity, he and his men were able to besiege and defeat a number of castles. A contemporary source reports such tactics as attaching burning resin to birds, setting the castle ablaze, and feigning reluctance to f
2 CONC ight, only to launch an attack at the most advantageous moment. Harald was able to build a large fortune in plunder from his victories.
2 CONT
2 CONT Harald was killed at the Battle of Stamford Bridge, outside the city of York, England. The battle took place on 25 September 1066. Harald died fighting against the forces of King Harold Godwinson of England. He had come to England
2 CONC  with the idea of claiming the English Throne as his own. He based this claim on a supposed agreement between Magnus and Harthacanute whereby if either died without heir, the other would inherit both England and Norway.
2 CONT
2 CONT He landed in Northern England with a force of around 15,000 men and 300 longboats (50 men in each boat), and had won a great victory on 20 September against the first English forces he met at the Battle of Fulford two miles south 
2 CONC of York. Some speculate that Harald's defeat at the Battle of Stamford Bridge was the result of his belief that King Harold Godwinson was prepared to surrender. This was not the case, and Harald's army was destroyed at Stamford Br
2 CONC idge. His army was so heavily beaten that less than 25 of the 300 recorded longboats Harald used to transport his forces to England were used to carry the survivors back to Norway. Not long after his victory over King Harald, Haro
2 CONC ld Godwinson was defeated by William the Conqueror at the Battle of Hastings. The fact that Harold had to make a forced march against Hardrada, fight at Stamford Bridge and then move at utmost speed back south to meet the Norman i
2 CONC nvasion, all in a matter of days, is widely seen as a primary factor in William's hard-fought victory at Hastings.
2 CONT
2 CONT Harald was the last great Viking king of Norway and his invasion of England and death at the Battle of Stamford Bridge in 1066 proved a true watershed moment. It marked the end of the Viking age. In Norway, Harald's death also mar
2 CONC ked the beginning of the Christian era: the Middle Ages. His body was taken to Trondheim in 1067 and buried in the Church of St. Mary. About 100 years later his body was reinterred in Helgeseter monastery which was demolished some
2 CONC time in the 1600s
1 NOTE Harald III
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=63d30218-8990-48be-b306-3cad8359fef4&tid=10145763&pid=-332977920
1 NOTE Harald III
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=63d30218-8990-48be-b306-3cad8359fef4&tid=10145763&pid=-332977920
1 NOTE     * OCCUPATION: Konge 1046-1066
2 CONT     * BIRTH: 1015, Ringerike
2 CONT     * DEATH: 25 Sep 1066, Stamford Bridge, Yorkshire, England
2 CONT     * BURIAL: Elgesæter kloster, Trondheim 
1 NOTE Harald Sigurtharson - aka Harald Haardraade - was describe
