1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abraham_Brodersson '''Abraham Brodersson'''] (ca. 1350 – 27 August 1410) was a Swedish statesman and military commander who lived in the 14th and early 15th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''He was a very able''' military leader and trusted advisor who contributed greatly to the rise of Queen [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margaret_I_of_Denmark Margaret I of Denmark], although initially he might have been introduce
2 CONC d to the Queen and raised in her favors due to his handsome appearance. He is reported to have had a daughter with Margaret.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''As a member of one''' of the richest and most powerful families in Sweden, the Baad family from Halland, he joined the pro-Margaret party in his country and helped her to depose [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert,_King_of_Sw
2 CONC eden Albert of Mecklenburg]. He then supported Margaret in uniting the three crowns of Scandinavia and designating as her heir Eric of Pomerania.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''He led Margaret's''' land forces and fleet together with [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Algot_Magnuson_of_Revsnes Algot Magnusson] in sieges of Stockholm during 1393-1394. He also participated in the campaign against the Island
2 CONC  of Gothland a decade later.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''On Trinity Sunday''', 17 July 1397, Margaret caused her grand nephew Eric to be crowned, and on the same day conferred knighthood on 133 people, among them Abraham Brodersson. An old chronicle recounts that "''Amidst all the fi
2 CONC ne folk present, none shone in manly beauty and bravery of apparel like Abraham Broderson ... Abraham was always present in her mind''." 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From 1403 to 1405''' Brodersson was the castellan of the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalmar_Castle Kalmar Castle]. He was also District Councillor of Halland, Värend and Finnveden. He became powerfully favored by her, and ab
2 CONC out him the people sang a lay "''The whole forest is full of Abraham''."
2 CONT
2 CONT '''He was one of the''' largest and richest landowners in the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalmar_Union Kalmar Union], with much property in Småland, Västergötland and Denmark. In Sweden, he had more servants than the king . Acc
2 CONC ording to Ahlbäck and Helle, as the castellan of the Kalmar Castle (a powerful office), he was notorious for levying heavy taxes, and he abused Margaret's trust in unashamedly acquiring lands from widows and petty nobility, possib
2 CONC ly amassing some 500 holdings. He became the owner of a lot of property through his first wife Margareta as well. The total number of farms Abraham owned might be more than 600.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In 1410, Queen Margaret''' appointed her nephew, King Eric of Pomerania, as the commander-in-chief in a campaign against Holstein. Based on abilities and experience, Abraham Brodersson should have been made the commander, but M
2 CONC argaret wanted to use this opportunity to reform and train her heir, who had been a disappointment to her. Thus, Abraham Brodersson was given a battalion, while the campaign was led by an untested and untried Eric to disastrous re
2 CONC sults. Resentful of Abraham Brodersson's presence and his power, after a failed siege against the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S%C3%B8nderborg_Castle Sønderborg Castle], Eric blamed his defeat on Abraham Brodersson, (possibly fa
2 CONC lsely) accused him of raping a woman and had him executed by sword, unknown to Margaret. According to[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_Gabriel_Michaud Louis Gabriel Michaud], by this time, Abraham Brodersson had become so power
2 CONC ful and was held by the queen in such high regards that he became a rival who threatened Eric's throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Abraham Brodersson has''' been praised for his bravery and military skills in such documents as the Rhyme chronicles, but also has a reputation for greed, brutality and dissolute life. Margaret lamented the death of her favouri
2 CONC te knight greatly (although, the patient Queen again forgave Eric for this act of treachery) and caused an altar for him to be raised in the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lund_Cathedral Cathedral of Lund], where masses for him an
2 CONC d herself would be held conjointly, and did not long survive him. The two years between his death and her own death were marked with great turbulence in Denmark, where people were heavily taxed to support the war Eric was waging. 
2 CONC The Queen though tried to keep her composure, took it upon herself to direct the Holstein-Schleswig war and was bringing it to a promising conclusion by diplomatic means when she suddenly died (some authors speculate that Abraham'
2 CONC s death might have contributed to it), leaving the war to Eric who made a disastrous situation out of it again.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Marriages and descendants===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''He married Margareta''' Petersdotter Dudde in 1382. The couple had one child named Birgitta (Brita). The girl's biological mother is suspected to be Queen Margaret. Brita accompagnied Queen Margaret to Vadstena where the fair y
2 CONC oung child, who apparently bore great resemblance to the Queen and certified by Margaret as having royal blood, was educated in a convent. Later, Brita married Thure Bjelke and gave birth to the first wife of King [https://en.wiki
2 CONC pedia.org/wiki/Charles_VIII_of_Sweden Charles VIII of Sweden] and thus was the ancestress of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christina_Gyllenstierna Christina Gyllenstierna]. After his first wife's death, he married Cecilia Nilsdot
2 CONC ter Jaernskaegg. He reportedly had many children born out of wedlock.
2 CONT <br/>
2 CONT <br/>
