2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Eivind Skaldespiller 
2 CONT * Chief and farmer. Parents: Skald Øyvind Finnsson Shellfisher (ca. 920 – ca. 990); the mother unknown. Married to Ragnhild Arnesdatter, daughter of Arne Arnmodsson and Tora Torsteinsdatter.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Chapter 3.    DESCENDANTS of FIN SKJALGE=
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Project MedLands Norway Nobility'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''FIN Skjalge,''' son of --- . 
2 CONT '''married GUNHILD Halfdansdatter, daughter of HALFDAN Jarl & his wife Ingeborg of Norway [illegitimate daughter of HARALD I "Harfagri" King of Norway].'''  Snorre names Gunhild as the daughter of Halfdan & his wife[65].  Snorre n
2 CONC ames Gunhild, daughter of Earl Halfdan and Ingebjorg, as the mother of Harek's father[66]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Fin Skjalge & his wife GUNHILD Halfdansdatter had two children'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1.         '''EYVIND Skaldaspiller''' (-990).  Pöt.  m ---.  The name of Eyvind´s wife is not known.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Eyvind & his wife had one child'''
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>* a)         '''HAREK of Thjotta''' (-murdered 1040).  '''married  RAGNHILD Arnesdatter, daughter of ARNE Arnmodsson & his wife Thora Thorsteinsdatter.'''  Snorre names Ragnhild, daughter of "Arne Arnmodson and Thora, Thorstei
2 CONC n Galge's daughter" as the wife of Harek of Thjotta[67].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Harek of Thjotta & his wife RAGNHILD Arnesdatter had three children'''
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>>* i)          '''EINAR Fluga of Thjotta'''
2 CONT >>>>>>* ii)         '''FIN Herkesson''' (-after 1028).  m ---.  The name of Fin´s wife is not known.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Fin Herkesson & his wife had one child'''
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>>>>* (a)       '''HAAKON Finsson''' m ---.  The name of Haakon´s wife is not known.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Haakon & his wife had one child'''
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>>>>>>* (1)       '''ULVHILD Haakonsdatter''' (-before 1143).  Fagrskinna names “Úlfhildr dróttning, dóttir Hákonar Finnssunar Hárekssunar or Þjóttu” as mother of “Karl konungs”, adding that she had first married “Nikolás Dana
2 CONC konungr”, secondly “Ingi Sviakonungr Hallsteinssunr” and thirdly “Sverkir konungr Kolssunr”[68].  Her second marriage is referred to by Saxo Grammaticus.  She was secretly abducted by Sverker I King of Sweden but their "connection
2 CONC  was accepted as a marriage"[69].  '''married firstly as his second wife, INGE II Halstensson King of Sweden, son of HALSTEN Stenkilsson King of Sweden & his wife''' --- (-1118).  '''married secondly (divorced) as his second wife,
2 CONC  NIELS King of Denmark, illegitimate son of SVEND II King of Denmark & his mistress --- (-murdered Schleswig 25 Jun 1134).''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>>* iii)        '''SVEN Harkesson'''
2 CONT * 2.         '''NJAL Finsson''' m ---.  The name of Njal´s wife is not known.  
2 CONT
2 CONT ''' NJAL Finsson & his wife had one child'''
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>>>>* a)         '''ASTRID Njalsdatter''' married RAGNVALD "the Old", son of ---. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source''' Project MedLands Norway Nobility - https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/NORWEGIAN%20NOBILITY.htm#FinArnessondied1062
2 CONT
2 CONT '''[http://www.geni.com/people/Snorri-Sturluson/3250062 Snorre], [http://www.geni.com/people/Olav-II-Haraldsson-%C2%ABthe-Saint-Digre%C2%BB-den-Hellige/6000000001200091331 Olav den Helliges] [http://no.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olav_den_
2 CONC helliges_saga saga], avsnitt 104''':
2 CONT
2 CONT The Tjøtta family, Norwegian chief executive, attached to the town center Tjøtta in Alstahaug municipality, Nordland. The family's ancestor was Øyvind Lambe from Berle in Nordfjord. He was the father of Finn Skjalge, who inherited
2 CONC  the farm Sandnes furthest north of Alstahaug after his mother in the early 900's. Finn was married to Gunnhild (daughter of Halvdan Earl and according to the saga daughter daughter of Harald Hårfagre), and their son was Håkon the
2 CONC  good friend Øyvind Finnsson Skaldespiller. He was the father of Hårek from Tjøtta (about 965-1036), who bought Tjøtta and moved there. Hårek's son was Lennart Einar Håreksson Fluga. The family's estate belonged to the late 13th c
2 CONC entury Bjarkøyetet.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Farmer and Chief Hårek Øyvindsson At Tjøtta=
2 CONT
2 CONT '''By the NORWEGIAN BIOGRAPHICAL LEXICON'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Chief and farmer. Parents: Skald Øyvind Finnsson Shellfisher (ca. 920 – ca. 990); the mother unknown. Married to Ragnhild Arnesdatter, daughter of Arne Arnmodsson and Tora Torsteinsdatter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hrek on Tjøtta was one of the most powerful chiefs in Hålogaland; he was Olav Tryggvason's husband and one of the leaders in the battle against Olav Haraldsson at Stiklestad.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Snorre, hair was of royal lineage. His grandfather was Find Skjalg and grandmother Gunnhild was the grandson of Harald. His father should not have been particularly wealthy, even though he was "great-grandfather". Hår
2 CONC ek had to buy his own farm in Tjøtta, where the small farmers must have dominated, but Snorre says that he had in a short time taken over the whole island and built a large farm there. He became rich, was particularly wise, almost
2 CONC  cunning, and received great respect from other chieftains because of his lineage. He had already had a long time in the royal saga with the lucrative taxation and the trade monopoly on the Sami (existential labor) when he appeare
2 CONC d in the royal saga at Olav Tryggvason's time.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hårek's history in the royal sagas goes over a relatively long period, from 998 to 1036. Together with other chieftains from Hålogaland he gathered army against Olav Tryggvason when the king 998 would embark on a Christian journey
2 CONC  north. The king gave up this first voyage, but when he returned the following year, Hårek had turned and was the first of the hollow-eyed chiefs to be baptized, while others continued the resistance and suffered defeat. Hrek also
2 CONC  became Olav Tryggvason's husband and retained the lucrative "existential".
2 CONT
2 CONT Next to Tore Hund in Bjarkøy, Hårek appears in the first part of the 1000s as the most powerful chief in Hålogaland. While Tore Hund had the greatest power in the northern part, Hårek held a similar position in the south. It has b
2 CONC een claimed that these two "inherited" their power from the chargers. The detailed stories by Snorre about Hårek and Tore Hund can be read as standalone stories about the contradictions between the hollow-eyed chieftains and Olav 
2 CONC Haraldsson, woven into the royal saga in a masterful way. All the details, and not least the entirety of the narratives, suggest that there were separate genealogies about these two that Snorre used in his time.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Olav Haraldsson came to power, he deployed Åsmund Grankjellsson as his loom in Hålogaland next to Hårek. Thus, a division of power arose, which Hårrek was constantly opposed to. It ended in a full fight between the two. First
2 CONC , Åsmund chased Hårek's men from a hut on the Helgeland coast. Hårek apparently found himself in it, but later took revenge by burning inside Åsmund's father Grankjell with 30 men. Then quit Hårek to Danish King Knud the Great and
2 CONC  became his lenderman when King Olav fled the country 1028. But Åsmund eventually his revenge when he beat 1036 Hårek to death by King Magnus the goodness, ax.
2 CONT
2 CONT There will be a double symbolism in this action, as Hårek was also one of the leaders of the army that killed Magnus' father, Olav Haraldsson, during the battle of Stiklestad.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, it is more clear than anywhere else at Snorre that it was not the controversy about Christianity, but about who should have the political and economic power, which was crucial to the Holes' opposition to Olav Haraldsson a
2 CONC t Stiklestad. Just as Hårek had to share power with Åsmund Grankjellsson, Tore Hund had to share his upbringing with Karle from Langøya. It was these attempts to wing the old chiefs that caused the rebellion of the Holly Eyes to K
2 CONC ing Olav. Typically, it is Hårek, Tore Hund and Hårek's brother-in-law Kalv Arnesson from Trøndelag who Snorre refers to as the foremost leaders of the peasant army at Stiklestad.
2 CONT
