2 CONT
2 CONT http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00020198&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Judith of Brittany''' (982–1017) was the daughter of '''Conan I, Duke of Brittany''' and '''Ermengarde of Anjou''', and the mother of '''Robert the Magnificent'''.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the first wife of '''Richard the Good''', Duke of Normandy, whom she married in 996. They had six children:
2 CONT
2 CONT *Richard (c. 1002/4), duke of Normandy
2 CONT *Alice of Normandy (c. 1003/5), married Renaud I, Count of Burgundy
2 CONT *Robert (c. 1005/7), duke of Normandy
2 CONT *William (c. 1007/9), monk at Fécamp, d. 1025
2 CONT *Eleanor (c. 1011/3), married to Baldwin IV, Count of Flanders
2 CONT *Matilda (c. 1013/5), nun at Fecamp, d. 1033
2 CONT
2 CONT The duchess Judith died in 1017 and was buried in the abbey of Bernay, which she had founded.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT See http://trees.ancestry.com/tree/25067072/person/12798619207
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Judith of Brittany also called Judith of Rennes (982–1017) Duchess of Normandy from c. 1000 until her death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Judith, born in 982, was the daughter of Conan I, Duke of Brittany and Ermengarde-Gerberga of Anjou.[1] She was the mother of Robert I, Duke of Normandy and paternal grandmother of William the Conqueror.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a part of an important double marriage alliance between Normandy and Brittany first recorded by William of Jumièges.[3] In 996 her brother Geoffrey I, Duke of Brittany married Hawise of Normandy, daughter of Richard I, Duk
2 CONC e of Normandy while in c. 1000 Judith married Richard II, Duke of Normandy, Hawise's brother.[4] The duchess Judith died on 28 August 1017[1] and was buried in the abbey of Bernay, which she had founded in 1013.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Family[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Judith married Richard II, Duke of Normandy[6] c. 1000.[2] They had six children: Richard (c. 1002/4), duke of Normandy.[2] Alice of Normandy (c. 1003/5), married Renaud I, Count of Burgundy.[2] Robert (c. 1005/7), duke of Normand
2 CONC y.[2] William (c. 1007/9), monk at Fécamp, d. 1025.[2] Eleanor (c. 1011/3), married to Baldwin IV, Count of Flanders.[2] Matilda (c. 1013/5), nun at Fecamp, d. 1033. She died young and unmarried.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Portal icon Brittany portal 
2 CONT
