2 CONT Richard II (born 23 August 970, in Normandy, France – 28 August 1026, in Normandy), called the Good (French: Le Bon), was the eldest son and heir of Richard I the Fearless and Gunnora.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard succeeded his father as Duke of Normandy in 996 but the first five years of his reign were spent with Count Ralph of Ivry wielding power and putting down a peasant insurrection[2].
2 CONT
2 CONT When he took power he strengthened his alliance with the Capetians by helping Robert II of France against the duchy of Burgundy. He formed a new alliance with Brittany by marrying his sister Hawise to Geoffrey I, Duke of Brittany 
2 CONC and by his own marriage to Geoffrey's sister, Judith.
2 CONT
2 CONT He also repelled an English attack on the Cotentin Peninsula that was led by Ethelred II of England. He pursued a reform of the Norman monasteries.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard attempted to improve relations with England through his sister's marriage to King Ethelred, but she was strongly disliked by the English. However, this connection later gave his grandson, William the Conqueror, part of his
2 CONC  claim to the throne of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married firstly (996) Judith (982-1017), daughter of Conan I of Brittany, by whom he had the following issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Richard (c. 1002/4), duke of Normandy
2 CONT     * Alice (c. 1003/5), married Renaud I, Count of Burgundy
2 CONT     * Robert (c. 1005/7), duke of Normandy
2 CONT     * William (c. 1007/9), monk at Fécamp, d. 1025
2 CONT     * Eleanor (c. 1011/3), married to Baldwin IV, Count of Flanders
2 CONT     * Matilda (c. 1013/5), nun at Fecamp, d. 1033
2 CONT
2 CONT Secondly he married Poppa of Envermeu, by whom he had the following issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Mauger (c. 1019), Archbishop of Rouen
2 CONT     * William (c. 1020/5), count of Arques
2 CONT
