1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 1-20792
1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 1-20792
2 CONT 1  _FA1 2  PLAC Duke of Normandy 2  SOUR S03227 3  DATA 4  TEXT Date of Import: Jul 13, 2000
2 CONT
2 CONT 1  NOTE Robert contributed to the restoration of Henry King of France tohis throne, and received from the gratitude of that monarch, the Vexin, asan additional to his patrimonial domains. In the 8th year of his reign,curiosity or 
2 CONC devotn induced him to undertake a pilgrimage to the Holy Land, wherethe fatiges of the journey and the heat of the climate so impaired hisconsitution he died on his way home.
2 CONT
2 CONT Known as Robert "The Devil" & Robert "The Magnificent". Count/ 6th Duke of Normandy 1027-1035
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: RC 89, 131; Kraentzler 1156, 1218, 1241, 1264, 1265, 1342,1350; Coe; Dukes; AF; K & Q of Britain; Norr; A. Roots 121, 121E, 130; France, Vol. 1 (1868), by M. Guizot and Madame Guizot de Witt; AIS; Davis.
2 CONT Roots: Robert I, Duke of Normandy. Married (Danish wife) Arlette (or Herleve). Left issue by this mistress.
2 CONT K: Robert I "le Magnifique ou le Diable." Count d'Hieme. Duke ofNormandy. In line 1350 he calls him erroneously calls him Richard I.
2 CONT Robert was called both "The Magnificent" and also "Robert the Devil," because of his "reckless and violent deeds of audacity, whetherin private life or in warlike expeditions." In 1034 or 1035, after a "fairlife from the political
2 CONC  point of view, but one full of turbulence and moral irregularity" he undertook a pilgrimage, barefoot, to Jerusalem "toexpiate his sins..." The Norman prelates and barons urged him not to go because they believed he might die on 
2 CONC the pilgrimage (they were right) and he had no successor. Robert, therefore, named his bastard son, William, as hissuccessor and prevailed upon King Henry I of France to sanction the arrangement--a decision the king was later to r
2 CONC egret.
2 CONT Guizot says Robert I was the fifth in succession from Rollo, the firstruler of Normandy. William was named after William Longsword, the son andsuccessor of Rollo. So the Norman reign went from Rollo, to William Longsword,Richard I
2 CONC , Richard II, Richard III, Robert I and William, who would become Williamthe Conqueror and, subsequently, William I, King of England.
2 CONT Richard III and Robert I were sons of Richard II, according to a chartin Butler.
2 CONT Robert instructed Herluin (another spelling) de Conteville to marryhis mistress, Harlette, if he failed to return from the Crusade. Herluin didso, and they had children.
2 CONT Dukes says Robert's last years were marked by signs of "eccentricity,if not unsoundness of mind."
2 CONT AIS: Robert, Duke of Normandy, born about 995, Normandy, France; died2 July 1035, Nicaea, Turkey.
