1 NOTE Robert contributed to the restoration of Henry King of France to histhrone,
2 CONT    and received from the gratitude of that monarch, the Vexin, as anadditional
2 CONT    to his patrimonial domains. In the 8th year of his reign, curiosity ordevotn
2 CONT    induced him to undertake a pilgrimage to the Holy Land, where thefatiges of
2 CONT    the journey and the heat of the climate so impaired his consitution hedied
2 CONT    on his way home.
2 CONT    Some sources call him Robert I the Magnificent!
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert I, byname ROBERT THE MAGNIFICENT, or THE DEVIL, French ROBERT LEMAGNIFIQUE, or LE DIABLE (d. July 1035, Nicaea), Duc de Normandie(1027-35), the younger son of Richard II of Normandie and the father, byhis mistress Arlette, 
2 CONC of William the Conqueror of England. On the deathof his father (1026/27), Robert contested the duchy with his elderbrother Richard III, legally the heir, until the latter's opportune deatha few years later. A strong ruler, Robert 
2 CONC succeeded in exacting theobedience of his vassals. On the death of Robert II the Pious, king ofFrance (1031), a crisis arose over the succession to the French throne.The Duke gave his support to Henry I against the party favouring
2 CONC  hisyounger brother; in reward for his services he de Manded and received theVexin Français, a territory not far north of Paris. A patron of themonastic reform movement, he died while returning from a pilgrimage toJerusalem. [Ency
2 CONC clopaedia Britannica CD '97][FAVthomas.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT    Byname Robert The Magnificent, or The Devil, French Robert LeMagnifique, or Le Diable duke of Normandie (1027/35), the younger son ofRichard II of Normandie and the father, by his mistress Arlette, ofWilliam the Conqueror of En
2 CONC gland. On the death of his father (1026/27),Robert contested the duchy with his elder brother Richard III, legallythe heir, until the latter's opportune death a few years later. A strongruler, Robert succeeded in exacting the obed
2 CONC ience of his vassals. On thedeath of Robert II the Pious, king of France (1031), a crisis arose overthe succession to the French throne. The Duke gave his support to Henry Iagainst the party favouring his younger brother; in rewar
2 CONC d for hisservices he demanded and received the Vexin Français, a territory not farnorth of Paris. A patron of the monastic reform movement, he died whilereturning from a pilgrimage to Jerusalem.
2 CONT
2 CONT To cite this page: "Robert I" Encyclopædia Britannica
2 CONT  <http://www.britannica.com/eb/article?idxref=274099>
