1 NAME Ingvar Igorevich, prince of Ryazan
1 NAME Ингварь Игоревич /Рязанский/
2 GIVN Ингварь Игоревич
2 SURN Рязанский
2 NICK в крещении Козьма
1 NAME Ингварь Игоревич, князь Рязанский
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1190
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1235
1 OCCU Рязанский князь (1217—1235)
1 TITL prince
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Ingvar Igorevich''', baptized as Kozma (d. 1235) — prince of Ryazan from 1217 to 1235.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1207, during the campaign of Vsevolod the Big Nest to the south, he was slandered by the children of Vladimir Glebovich in treason and, together with other Ryazan princes, was sent to prison by Vsevolod, from which he was relea
2 CONC sed only by Vsevolod's successor, Yuri, in 1212.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1217, Gleb Vladimirovich wanted to kill his cousins, including the Igorevichs, by cunning, and for this purpose invited them to a congress in Isady. Ingvar Igorevich escaped death by not coming to congress, while his brothers G
2 CONC leb (?) and Roman died. The chronicle does not report the time and circumstances of the occupation of the Ryazan table by Ingvar, but in 1219 he defeated Gleb and the Polovtsians brought by him, then, asking for help from the Gran
2 CONC d Duke of Vladimir Yuri Vsevolodovich, defeated them again. Gleb fled to the Polovtsians and went crazy, Konstantin 20 years later participated in the struggle for Galich on the side of Rostislav of Chernigov.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1220, not far from Ryazan, he founded the Olgov Monastery.
2 CONT
2 CONT The version available in historiography that Ingvar Igorevich died in 1235 most likely comes from Tatishchev, but she does not have any supporting evidence from other monuments. At the same time, there is news from the Resurrectio
2 CONC n Chronicle under 1217 “and according to Ingvar, the prince’s children are Roman, Yury, Oleg,” and in 1237, indeed, they already reigned.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a problem - is Ingvar Igorevich one person with the son Ingvar Ingvarevich attributed to him, according to some researchers, a "dynastic phantom". This "son" is believed to appear in 1237 during the ruin of Ryazan by Batu
2 CONC . If the identification of "father" and "son" as one prince is correct, and if Ingvar Igorevich lived until 1237, then it was Ingvar Igorevich who was a participant in these tragic events.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the First Novgorod Chronicle, at the time of the advent of Batu, Prince Yuri Igorevich was sitting in Ryazan - the brother of Ingvar Igorevich (the "uncle" of Ingvar Ingvarevich). Apparently, Ingvar was absent from th
2 CONC e city at the time of the attack. “In this state of affairs, it turns out to be possible to conditionally identify Prince Ingvar Ingorevich, a character in the late Ryazan story, with Ingvar Igorevich, the son of Igor Glebovich, w
2 CONC ho returns to the city devastated by the Tatars and mourns his relatives.”
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family==
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Igor Glebovich
2 CONT
2 CONT Brothers:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Roman Igorevich (d. 1217) - principal prince of Ryazan.
2 CONT * Yuri Igorevich - specific prince of Ryazan 1207, presumably Grand Duke of Ryazan 1235-1237.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Yuri? - presumably the Grand Duke of Ryazan (1235-dec.1237) - '''actually it's his brother, not son''' 
2 CONT # Roman (killed in 1238) - a participant in the battle of Kolomna.
2 CONT # Oleg Krasny (d. 1258) - Grand Duke of Ryazan (1252-1258).
2 CONT # Evdokia (d. 1278) - the wife of the 1st Prince of Uglich Vladimir Konstantinovich.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Resurrection Chronicle under 1217, the listing of the Ingvarevichs Roman, Yuri, and Oleg indicates the seniority of Roman.
2 CONT
2 CONT "The Tale of the Devastation of Ryazan by Batu" also mentions his son, Prince Ingvar Ingvarevich (for a possible historiographical error, see above).
