2 CONT Eric "X" of Sweden, Swedish: Erik Knutsson; Old Norse: Eiríkr Knútsson (c. 1180 – 1216) was the King of Sweden between 1208 and 1216. He was the son of Knut Eriksson and his queen of an unknown name, who probably was Cecilia Johan
2 CONC sdotter. He was born around 1180 in Eriksberg royal manor. Referring to Erik Knutsson as King Eric X is a later invention, counting backwards from Eric XIV (1560–68). He and his brother Charles IX (1604–1611) adopted numerals acco
2 CONC rding to a fictitious history of Sweden. The amount of Swedish monarchs named Eric before Eric XIV (at least seven) is unknown, going back into prehistory, and none of them used numerals. It would be speculative to try to affix a 
2 CONC mathematically accurate one to this king. When Eric's father, King Canute I, died peacefully in 1195, all his sons were only children. Eric apparently was not the eldest of them. Due to the influence of the mighty second-of-the-re
2 CONC alm, Jarl Birger Brosa, Sverker II, the head of the rival dynasty was chosen as King of Sweden, over the underaged boys. King Canute's sons continued to live in the Swedish royal court, until 1203, when his brothers and family bro
2 CONC ught forward claims to the throne, and Sverker did not acquiesce, at which point Eric and his brothers escaped to Norway. In 1205, the brothers returned to Sweden with Norwegian support, but lost the Battle of Älgarås, where three
2 CONC  of Eric's brothers were killed. In 1208 Eric returned to Sweden with Norwegian troops and defeated Sverker in the Battle of Lena. Eric became thus chosen the king of Sweden. Sverker attempted to reconquer the throne, but was defe
2 CONC ated and killed in Battle of Gestilren in 1210. The banner under which King Eric's troops fought, was preserved by his kinsman the lawspeaker Eskil Magnusson of the Bjelbo clan in Skara, who in 1219 gave it as honorary to his visi
2 CONC ting Icelandic colleague Snorre Sturlasson. At that time, king Eric X married princess Richeza of Denmark, daughter of the late Valdemar I of Denmark, and sister of the then reigning Valdemar II the Victorious. This was to make up
2 CONC  relations with Denmark, which had traditionally supported the Sverker dynasty, against the Norwegian-supported dynasty of Eric. Eric X was the first Swedish king who was crowned. He died suddenly in fever in 1216 in the castle of
2 CONC  Näs on the island of Visingsö. He was buried in the Varnhem Abbey Church.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eric_X_of_Sweden
2 CONT
2 CONT Eric "X" of Sweden, Swedish: Erik Knutsson; Old Norse: Eiríkr Knútsson (c. 1180 – 1216) was the King of Sweden between 1208 and 1216. He was the son of Knut Eriksson and his queen of an unknown name, who probably was Cecilia Johan
2 CONC sdotter. He was born around 1180 in Eriksberg royal manor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Referring to Erik Knutsson as King Eric X is a later invention, counting backwards from Eric XIV (1560–68). He and his brother Charles IX (1604–1611) adopted numerals according to a fictitious history of Sweden. The amount of Swed
2 CONC ish monarchs named Eric before Eric XIV (at least seven) is unknown, going back into prehistory, and none of them used numerals. It would be speculative to try to affix a mathematically accurate one to this king.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Eric's father, King Canute I, died peacefully in 1195, all his sons were only children. Eric apparently was not the eldest of them. Due to the influence of the mighty second-of-the-realm, Jarl Birger Brosa, Sverker II, the he
2 CONC ad of the rival dynasty was chosen as King of Sweden, over the underaged boys.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Canute's sons continued to live in the Swedish royal court, until 1203, when his brothers and family brought forward claims to the throne, and Sverker did not acquiesce, at which point Eric and his brothers escaped to Norway.
2 CONC  In 1205, the brothers returned to Sweden with Norwegian support, but lost the Battle of Älgarås, where three of Eric's brothers were killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1208 Eric returned to Sweden with Norwegian troops and defeated Sverker in the Battle of Lena. Eric became thus chosen the king of Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sverker attempted to reconquer the throne, but was defeated and killed in Battle of Gestilren in 1210. The banner under which King Eric's troops fought, was preserved by his kinsman the lawspeaker Eskil Magnusson of the Bjelbo cla
2 CONC n in Skara, who in 1219 gave it as honorary to his visiting Icelandic colleague Snorre Sturlasson.
2 CONT
2 CONT At that time, king Eric X married princess Richeza of Denmark, daughter of the late Valdemar I of Denmark, and sister of the then reigning Valdemar II the Victorious. This was to make up relations with Denmark, which had tradition
2 CONC ally supported the Sverker dynasty, against the Norwegian-supported dynasty of Eric.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eric X was the first Swedish king who was crowned.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died suddenly in fever in 1216 in the castle of Näs on the island of Visingsö. He was buried in the Varnhem Abbey Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family His marriage produced several daughters, at least three and possibly as many as five, and one and only son, born posthumously, the future Eric XI of Sweden. Daughters include Märta (contested by Dick Harrison), married with
2 CONC  Marshal Nils Sixtensson (Sparre), and Ingeborg of Sweden, possibly the youngest daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some later pretensions claim that one of his daughters would have been Marianna, who married a duke of Pomerania, becoming an ancestress of the House of Mecklenburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is also a ancestor of prince Philip, the current husband of Queen Elizabeth II.
2 CONT
