2 CONC . When Halvard Jonsen Smør was born in 1327 in Bergen, Norway, his father, Jon, was 87 and his mother, Ulvhild, was 72. He died in 1391 in Oslo, Norway, at the age of 64. Hallvard Jonson Smør (fl. 1368–1372) was a Norwegian knight
2 CONC .Hallvard was a son of Jon Smør. In 1368 Hallvard owned Røken and Hurum in Oslo syssel, which Gaute Eriksen of the Galte-family had owned the year before. He later gave land in "Refvolom" in Romerike for a Requiem Mass, which was 
2 CONC confirmed by King Haakon VI of Norway in 1372. He was married to an otherwise unknown sister of Svale Ølversson, an "Ølversdotter", and had two known sons, Jon and Hallkjell.
2 CONT
2 CONT Smør, or after the coat of arms, "Leopard's head (under chevron)", was the name of a Norwegian medieval family of the high nobility.[1] The family was one of the few original noble families of Norway, as it, unlike many other fami
2 CONC lies, did not originate from Denmark or Sweden. The family owned land in Norway, as well as on the Faroe Islands and Shetland (which at the time was a Norwegian overseas possession). The male line of the family died out in the lat
2 CONC e 15th century.
