2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Noter af Holger Hertzum:
2 CONT Hr. Anders Jachimsen eller Jacobsen til Voersgaard (Hellum Herred) mødte 1449 på Skiftet efter Farfaderen, var 1460 Ridder og da bl.a. nærværende i Aarhus ved Forliget mellem de Rosenkrantzer og Lave Brock, gav 1466 sammen med sin
2 CONC  Frue Gods til Helligaands Huset i Aalborg, holdt 1475 skifte med sine Fættere, var 1481 Rigsråd, beseglede 1487 Hyldingen i Lund, var forlehnet med Hellum Herred, gjorde 1490 sit testamente og døde efter rigelig at have betænkt K
2 CONC irker og Klostre. Gift med Anne Lauridsdatter Muus ( med et Møllehjul i Vaabnet) til Stenalt (Rugsø Herred); de havde 1467 udset sig en Begravelse i Randers Klosterkirke, men ligger nu i Stenalts Sognekirke Ørsted, 1492 holdtes Sk
2 CONC ifte efter dem.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ejer af Randrup (Skibsted):
2 CONT
2 CONT (1490) Anders Jacobsen Bjørn
2 CONT
2 CONT (1490-1509) Jes Andersen Mus Bjørn 
2 CONT
2 CONT (1509) Ingerd Andersdatter Bjørn gift (1) Munk (2) Seefeld
2 CONT
2 CONT (1509-1521) Niels Jensen Munk
2 CONT
2 CONT (1521) Ingerd Andersdatter Bjørn gift (1) Munk (2) Seefeld
2 CONT
2 CONT (1521-1538) Jens Tygesen Seefeld
2 CONT
2 CONT Randrup er en gammel landsbyhovedgård, som nævnes første gang i 1490. Gården ligger i Skibsted Sogn, i det tidligere Hellum Herred, Ålborg Amt, Skørping Kommune, nu Rebild Kommune. Hovedbygningen er opført i 1550-1570-1735, tilbyg
2 CONC get i 1840 og ombygget i 1865-1869, brændt i 1976 og genopført i 1978 ved arkitekt John Sørensen
2 CONT
2 CONT Randrup Gods er på 251 hektar
2 CONT
2 CONT http://da.wikipedia.org/wiki/Randrup_(Skibsted_Sogn)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Randrup var oprindl. en Udflytterby fra Skibsted. Et Tingsvidne fra 1396 nævner en Gaard „i" Randrup. Ved Slutn. af 15. Aarh. ejedes Halvdelen af R. af Vor Frue Kloster i Aalborg, Halvdelen af Hr. Anders Jacobsen (Bjørn) til Vorgd
2 CONC . og Stenalt, efter hvis Død (omtr. 1490) der af R. oprettedes en Sædegaard for hans 3. Søn Jep Andersen Mus. Efter hans Død (omtr. 1509) tilfaldt den hans Søster Inger, g, 1. m. Niels Jensen (kaldet Munk Jensen) af Visborg, 2. m.
2 CONC  Jens Thygesen (Seefeld) af Dalsgd. Sidstnævnte skrev sig til R. 1523 og 1532. R. og Visborggd.
2 CONT  ejedes derefter af Sønnen Enevold Jensen, † 1557, hans Enke Fru Agathe Pogwisch, 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://runeberg.org/trap/3-4/0504.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Vorgaard's history (Bælum parish), Series: From Himmerland and Kjær Herred, Volume: 1918-1920
2 CONT Publication: 1918-1920
2 CONT Citation details: page 2
2 CONT Note: He was married to Anne Laurensdatfer Muus (with a wheel in the Vaabnet) to Stenalt (the town of Randers), which farm she had only inherited after her father Lauretis Muus and where they lived most time.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Anders Jacobsen (Bjørn) to Vorgård and Stenalt. Jyske Gods collectors around 1500, Series: From Himmerland and Kjær Herred, Volume: 1987
2 CONT Publication: 1987
2 CONT Citation details: page 101
2 CONT Note:
2 CONT In the letter we meet Anders Jakobsen for the first time in 1441. The author and monks of the Randers monastery confirm in the letter that they are to hold a trade fair for "Mr. Anders Jakobsen, the knight of Vorgård, and his wife
2 CONC , Mrs Anne Lauritsdatters (Mus), their children and parents' souls, alive and dead, and for all Christian souls "as well as 4 extensions (soulmates) annually. The text is interesting in several circumstances. It is partly very ear
2 CONC ly compared to the other information we have about him. The next two letters referring to him are from 1449 and 1460, and only after that the flow of letters begins to flow closer. Furthermore, it shows that he was already the own
2 CONC er of Vorgård in 1441, was married to Anne Mus, had children and carefully looked after all the family members' souls. We will soon see these conditions. First of all let us know that he was also a knight. In those times, that tit
2 CONC le said much about a nobleman's social position. The title was awarded to many of the people we usually refer to the high-end circle, though not all, while it was very rare that the knights were awarded to people who were lower in
2 CONC  social terms. Anders Jakobsen must have achieved the title titled very early.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death	about 1480 (age 65 years)
2 CONT Stenalt, Ørsted, Rougsø, Randers
2 CONT
2 CONT  Source: Vorgaard's history (Bælum parish), Series: From Himmerland and Kjær Herred, Volume: 1918-1920
2 CONT Publication: 1918-1920
2 CONT Citation details: page 3
2 CONT Burial	about 1480 (on the date of death)
2 CONT Ørsted Church, Ørsted, Rougsø, Randers
2 CONT
2 CONT  Source: Vorgaard's history (Bælum parish), Series: From Himmerland and Kjær Herred, Volume: 1918-1920
2 CONT Publication: 1918-1920
2 CONT Citation details: page 3
2 CONT
2 CONT Note:
2 CONT Anders Jacobsen had bought a funeral for himself and his wife in the Franciscan monastery in Randers, which was not used, however, as they were in their parish church. Your tombstone, formerly found in the thunder room, is depicte
2 CONC d in the National Museum Archives. Herpaa is seen> carved pictures of a knight in armor with a wife, even a wide folded suit. Along the edge of the stone is the following inscription :. , Pie • iacet hns / anhreas • iacobi • mifes
2 CONC  • be • ftenoit / cu • o ~ jore hna • anna • a: ii.o • bni • [m] cccc • lxxxx • lxxx (Here is Mr. Anders Andersen, Knight, to Stenalt with Mrs. Anna Anna. In the Lord's Age 1490, 80.) Anders Jacobsen Bjorn is abandoned to be dead 
2 CONC 1490. The figure 80 must be the wife's Darsar in 1480, it appears to be an unusual way of giving a medieval Tombstone.
2 CONT
