0 @N6000000000129985398@ NOTE From Kymm Smoothy - Gisle GEIRLAUGSSON Seim was born to parentsprobably Geirlaug Gislesson and Unknown ca 1440 at Seim. He wasmarried to Unknown SEMMINGSDTR. Gisle was manager of Seim andwas probably the son of Geirlaug Gislesson.
1 CONC  He is named indocuments in 1484, 1485, 1496 and 1499. Gisle and Olav Bjornsendocumented that they were at Auli in Odalen when Amund Eliussongave 4 oyresbol in North Oyen in Odalen and a horn worth 4 marksto Halvard Gullesen. Amun
1 CONC d had been guardian for Halvard, andwith the transfer he had strengthened that account. 01.04.1484he was a lawman at Auli along with Olaff Biørnsson (DN III 947)07.03.1485 he was a lawman at Seim with Biorn Arnalson (DN V927): He 
1 CONC is also mentioned in 1496 and 1499. It is the feasiblethat Gisle later was at Auli in South Odal , as a Gisle wasfarming there in 1514 and 1528 - Auli was inherited by hisdescendants. Amund was a farmer at Seim in 1514 and 1528. G
1 CONC isleof Seim was also at Auli in 1499. In 1520 a Gisle was theemployer at Auli and an Amund at Seim. Gisle was probablymarried to a daughter of a Semming. Of their children we know: -Semming - farmer of Mellom Vaagstad in Nes - pro
1 CONC bably married toa daughter of Otter Iversen Aarstad in Oppstad. Halvor - farmerof Auli in Sor Odal, named in 1500 and 1514. Paal - farmer ofAarstad in Sor Odal, alive in 1572. Hans - farmer of Seim, alivein 1572. Ola - farmer in H
1 CONC aug in Nord Odal in 1567. Kaare - Whomwe know no more about. Later, one of Gisle Semmingsen's father'sbrothers, Hans Gislesen, became a farmer at Seim. He could havebought 15 lispund of land estate in Seim from GjorvilFadersdatter
1 CONC  at Kambo. He had a son named Amund Hansen whobecame a farmer at Ellingsrud, and his son, Gisle Amundsen tookover Ellingsrud between 1595 and 1600. King Kristian I's reignhad been a peaceful time for the Norwegian community. Howev
1 CONC er inthe last half of 1490's a wave of agitation swept over thecountry. In 1496 the farmers at Sunnmøre actually slayed thesupporters of the cabinet ministers and feudal overlord ArildKane and several of his Swedish supporters at 
1 CONC the Thing. A yearlater, in early autumn one of Knut Alvssons bailiffs, LasseSkjold, was beaten to death when he was helping Knut atRomerike. The murder of the bailiff came at a bad time, as kingHans, his feudal overlord and two ar
1 CONC med forces at this point wasdeeply engaged in negotiations with Sweden. So as soon asinnovation came to the king, he accordingly wrote a letter atonce to the commoners where he composed it and promised to cometo the country to giv
1 CONC e it law and order. The definite resolveabout the kings visit was comprised at a Danish - Norwegiancabinet ministers meeting in Copenhagen the following year. Theking had first laid plans to travel to Sweden in the New Year,but to
1 CONC ok a route around Båhus, so that he was there 25thNovember. The farmers were clever rebels. They believed they hadbeen exposed to grevious injustice for a long time. A fewmonth's after the bailiff's murder the commoners in the sou
1 CONC thpart of Gudbrandsdalen in Akershus and county of the king, wrotethanking him for his letter, and complaining about theirdestitution: It told that among the bailiffs there in thecounty, there were a few foreign men who did not kn
1 CONC ow thecountry's laws properly. Others gave no consideration to thedestitute, but took the matter lightly, and to the farmers itwas a huge matter that during the course was making the countypoor. It shouldn't be so, they believed, 
1 CONC that a man who had notcommitted an offence in his life could be fined more than whathe made, to pay the kings taxes. It had also occurred that thecastle masters had taken the letter off the men in the countystating that they had d
1 CONC one nothing different to what wasconsidered law and order there. Not all in the county were inagreeance that such a letter had been issued. There were manypoor men, both healers and scholars, who would certainly havegone and expre
1 CONC ssed his objections to him, but could not, manybecause of disease, others because of destitution. The empire'spublic decree regarding guests and the making out the expenseshad been rarely abided by of late. The king blamed himself
1 CONC accordingly for Gods guilt and sent a Dane to the country whomon his behalf could acquire them law as what was happening wasunjustified. Later the commoners in the Eidsvoll area also wroteto the king more times and told about Lass
1 CONC e Skjolds fiercetreatment of them: Then a man named Skase Hallvardsson cameforward and told the story how Lasse Skjold had bashed him threetimes with a warhammer, so he fell about stunned. When he wasable to stand again, the baili
1 CONC ff had beaten him two more timeswith the warhammer and said: " you will have three fallingdevils coming down on you. I shall put my sword through youanyplace I can find you." A Dane named Aslak Trondsson said thatLasse Skjold had 
1 CONC raped a girl. He had also visited another mannamed Per Ingemarsson and drowned him in a stream. Lasse Skjoldhad taken nine lodd of silver from a man named Anders Assersson.One man named Olav Arnesson was drunk and lay on the floor
1 CONC passed out. Then Lasse Skjold came and picked up the man andthrew him in on the floor again. The man lay inanimate on thefloor for a long time. In the morning the man was himself again.Then he was unlawfully made to give Lasse Skj
1 CONC old a skippund ofmalt and a cowhide. There could have been reason for him tonotice that the farmers depicted a picture of very longstressful situation between the commoners and the administrativeentities. They lamented over a few 
1 CONC of the castle masters. Theiractions were indirectly a strong character of self-defence. Thefarmer's agitation would not dissipate. In the middle ofDecember Knut Alvsson found himself needing to write to HenrikKrummedike for help. 
1 CONC All of the commoners in the district wererioting. He therefore invited Henrik to meet him in Borgesyslawith two hundred men. Together with double the strength theymight be able to pacify the commoners. This did not occur, asHenrik
1 CONC  Krummedike reasoned that the connection with the opposingsides was badly motivated from before that, so he now turned adeaf ear to it. Knut Alvsson had a short time beforehand takenconfiscation of a ship and any goods which inter
1 CONC ested him,allegedly to cover outstanding debts. He got a beautifulchestnut rake out of the fire himself. It is not hard to believethat Knut Alvsson estimated the situation as critical. Thecommoners at Nes and Ullenhov at Romerike 
1 CONC had sent him a letter,informing him how they were collectively standing in support ofthe man who had killed Lasse Skjold, Engelbrekt Gunnarsson, andhow they would not be beaten. Not long after that the commonerstook a new and dram
1 CONC atic initiative. From Nes at Romerike andOdalen a budkavle that was burnt on one end and had a hook inthe other was sent out as a message. It symbolised that thosewho did not follow it, would be punished with the fire andgallows. 
1 CONC This could have led to a dangerous confrontation atAkers Thing in the beginning of February, where large crowds ofpeople had met in the cause of the budkavle. The public officerswho were where this situation occurred, appeared dip
1 CONC lomatichowever, and hoped the commoners had no visible desire for ashowdown there and then. They therefore succeeded to get throughthe Thing except there arose a grevious occurrence. Probably theking's promise about coming to Norw
1 CONC ay to see about thecommoners' grievances was a very big cause for a fall out. Afurther showdown was shoved aside to the near future. King Hansarrived at Båhus as promised at the end of November 1498. In themeantime preparations we
1 CONC re being made for an ordinary lawsuitregarding what had occurred. The Law-Thing was organised byHenrik Krummedike and Jens Andersson Beidenak, who later saw arelatively unknown bishop in Odense. This was because the kinghad delega
1 CONC ted his court of appeal - and perogative of mercy. Thewhole four lawmen were called in to decide the correct questionsin presence of a large number of cabinet ministers. They hadduly put them in a closed court to afford the court 
1 CONC the maximumprestige and authority. The actual Law-Things negotiationshappened in Konghelle in the fortress' immediate vicinity. Theissue about Lasse Skjolds murderer was first taken up forjudgement. Knut Alvsson and, in accordance
1 CONC  with the kingscitation letter, nine farmers with lawyers on the private behalfof the commoners in Akershus County met for the trial. KnutAlvsson first took the stand and accused the commoners forhaving beaten his assistant, his b
1 CONC ailiff Lasse Skjold, when theyhad charged and assembled against him - (Knut Alvsson). Thecommoners' representatives answered, it appeared that theycertainly had a good reason for it, that "it was their huge needto". Both parties t
1 CONC hen put their cases forward for judgement.The adjudication demonstrated that Lasse Skjold's injustice wasso transparent and serious that they had to take considerationof them. The bailiff's muder was assessed as not outrightmurder
1 CONC . It was again a little concession. The penalty forcharging and assembling at Akers Thing was meanwhile unlawful inthe lawbook constitution. Those who had stayed back there couldall have been outlawed and have forgone their belong
1 CONC ings andtheir freedom. But after an urgent appeal to Knut Krummedike andJens Beidenak from the commoners' lawyer about this issue, thisalso became a part of the sentence, which involved a largeamount of money. Although the sentenc
1 CONC e in general was mild, theissue arose of the farmer's expensive fines. There were abouttwo hundred farmers who were convicted. Altogether, two monthslater they had paid out 20 marks and 2 skilling in cash, 319lodd silver and 292 m
1 CONC arks in copper. A year later, when thearrears were still coming in, it all amounted to another 70marks in brass, 20 lodd in silver and a number of cans, boilersand casseroles of copper and tin. But it is apparent that thefarmers a
1 CONC ll felt they had a good right to kill Lasse Skjold andfelt they had been hit hard even with the relatively mildsentence. It occurred that two or three of the commoners werebeheaded after requesting a meeting in Konghelle regarding
1 CONC  thefirst list of payments. It can hardly show anything differentother than once again they had stirred trouble about thesentence. There is also a note that more sources from thefollowing period spread information about continued 
1 CONC agitationamongst the commoners at Ostlandet. On Feb 2, 1499 GisleGelossen of Semme was fined 6 and 1/2 weight of silver for theLasse Skjold case with son Halvor of Auli who was fined 3 and1/2 weight of silver. Sources - DN XIII p.
1 CONC 162 DN III p. 947 DN Vp. 927
0 @N6000000000129988235@ NOTE Bl a fra Leif Mikalsen og Laila Normann Christiansen.
0 @N6000000000944918588@ NOTE (Research):177,666 GUNNULV (ERIKSEN?), possibly of Ringnes, Krødsherad, [born ca. 1330], perhaps son of EIRIK RINGNES (# 355,332) 
1 CONT
1 CONT  Children:
1 CONT
1 CONT  a) Helga Gunnulvsdatter, # 88,833 above
1 CONT
1 CONT [Gunnulv's patronymic is not given in contemporary documents.  A Gunnulv is mentioned in 1350, apparently connected with Lesteberg, Krødsherad.  However, there is no definite evidence that this Gunnulv was the father of Helga Gunn
1 CONC ulvsdatter, apparently the principal heiress of Ringnes farm.  Gunnulv may have been a son-in-law, rather than a son, of Eirik Ringnes who is named in 1325.  In a document said to be dated 1359 (but more probably should be dated 1
1 CONC 389), Halvard This and his daughter Gyrid deeded the west farm of Ringnes to Narve Rolvsen.  Halvard This may have been a son or son-in-law of Gunnulv.  The 1325 document names two men at Veikaaker farm:  Guttorm and Gudulv.  The 
1 CONC recurrence of the name Gudulv in the Ringnes family suggests that Gudulv at Veikaaker may have been an ancestor.  The precise link is unknown.]
