0 @N6000000000232604354@ NOTE ==========
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert the Magnificent
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert "The Magnificent",
1 CONT Duke of Normandy
1 CONT Robert the Magnificent as part of the Six Dukes of Normandy statue in the town square of Falaise.
1 CONT Born (June 22,1000)
1 CONT Normandy, France
1 CONT Died 3 July 1035
1 CONT Nicaea
1 CONT Office Duke of Normandy
1 CONT 1028-1035
1 CONT Preceded by Richard III
1 CONT Succeeded by William the Conqueror (William II)
1 CONT "Robert I of Normandy" redirects here. For his ancestor who took the baptismal name "Robert", see Rollo.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert the Magnificent[1] (June 22, 1000 – 3 July 1035), also called the Devil or Robert I, was the Duke of Normandy from 1027 until his death. He was the son of Richard II of Normandy and Judith, daughter of Conan I of Rennes. He
1 CONC  was the father of William the Conqueror.
1 CONT
1 CONT When his father died, his elder brother Richard succeeded, whilst he became Count of Hiémois. When Richard died a year later, there were great suspicions that Robert had Richard murdered, hence his other nickname, "Robert le diabl
1 CONC e" ("the devil"). He is sometimes identified with the legendary Robert the Devil.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert aided King Henry I of France against Henry's rebellious brother and mother, and for his help he was given the territory of the Vexin. He also intervened in the affairs of Flanders, supported Edward the Confessor, who was th
1 CONC en in exile at Robert's court, and sponsored monastic reform in Normandy.
1 CONT
1 CONT By his mistress, Herleva of Falaise, he was father of two children:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. the future William I of England (1028-1087)
1 CONT    2. Adelaide of Normandy (1030-c. 1083), who was married three times:
1 CONT          1. Enguerrand II, Count of Ponthieu
1 CONT          2. Lambert II, Count of Lens
1 CONT          3. Odo II of Champagne
1 CONT
1 CONT After making his illegitimate son William his heir, he set out on pilgrimage to Jerusalem. According to the Gesta Normannorum Ducum he travelled by way of Constantinople, reached Jerusalem, and died on the return journey at Nicaea
1 CONC  on 2 July 1035. Some sources attribute his death to poison and date it to 1 or 3 July. His son William, aged about eight, succeeded him.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the historian William of Malmesbury, around 1086 William sent a mission to Constantinople and Nicaea, charging it with bringing his father's body back to be buried in Normandy. Permission was granted, but, having trav
1 CONC elled as far as Apulia (Italy) on the return journey, the envoys learned that William himself had meanwhile died. They then decided to re-inter Robert's body in Italy.
1 CONT
1 CONT ==========
