0 @N6000000001783304675@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT Born circa 1008
1 CONT Acceded on August 6, 1027
1 CONT Died between July 1 and 3, 1035 at Nicaea in Bithynia while returning from a pilgrimage to the Holy Land. Ancient Nicaea was located about 65 miles south east of Constantinople. Referred to as Robert "the Devil" (Moriarty, Planata
1 CONC gent Ancestry, pg. 13), he is also known as Robert "the Magnificent".
1 CONT
1 CONT Relationship and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert had a girlfriend named Herleva. Both were under twenty, possibly as young as seventeen, when Herleva became pregnant with their first child who was to become William the Conqueror. Herleva's father was Fulbert, who was prob
1 CONC ably a tanner. Fulbert has been described as "polinctor" which translates more readily as "embalmer", or one who prepares corpses for burial, but the traditon is strong that he was a tanner; and Falaise, Normandy was famous for it
1 CONC s tanneries.
1 CONT Robert married in 1031 to Estrith, daughter of Sweyn Forkbeard, King of Denmark. They had a divorce in 1032. Estrith had been previously married to Robert's father, Richard "the Good" and they had a divorce after a year or so as w
1 CONC ell. We are left to wonder if Estrith, whose mother was known as "Sigrid the Haughty", was difficult to get along with.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert named named  William heir to the Dukedom of Normandy prior to his ill advised pilgrimmage. William's guardians were:
1 CONT Robert, Count of Évreux and Archbishop of Rouen (died on March 16, 937), William's great uncle.
1 CONT Alan (III), Count of Brittany, William's father's cousin.
1 CONT Gilbert "Crispin", Count of Brionne, also William's father's cousin.
1 CONT Osbern, Steward or Dapifer of Normandy, William's great grandmother's nephew.
1 CONT Turchetil (Turold), a respected official whose exact function in not understood.
1 CONT Walter, William's uncle (his mother's brother), who was never named as an official "tutor" but nonetheless saved William from assassins and murderers on several occasions.
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT
1 CONT In late 1034, curiosity or devotion induced Robert (I) "the Devil", 6th Duke of Normandy, to undertake a pilgrimage to the Holy Land, where the fatigues of the journey and the heat of the climate so impaired his consitution he die
1 CONC d at Nicaea (in the area of Iznik in modern Turkey) on his way home.
1 CONT http://www.robertsewell.ca/normandy.html#gen10
1 CONT
1 CONT Other Source
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert the Magnificent (June 22, 1000 - 3 July 1035), also called Robert the Devil or Robert I, was the Duke of Normandy from 1027 until his death. He was the son of Richard II of Normandy and Judith, daughter of Conan I of Rennes
1 CONC . He was the father of William the Conqueror.
1 CONT
1 CONT When his father died, his elder brother Richard succeeded, whilst he became Count of Hiémois. When Richard died a year later, there were great suspicions that Robert had Richard murdered, hence his other nickname, "Robert le diabl
1 CONC e" ("the devil"). He is sometimes identified with the legendary Robert the Devil.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert aided King Henry I of France against Henry's rebellious brother and mother, and for his help he was given the territory of the Vexin. He also intervened in the affairs of Flanders, supported Edward the Confessor, who was th
1 CONC en in exile at Robert's court, and sponsored monastic reform in Normandy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Relationship and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT By his mistress, Herleva of Falaise, he was father of two children:
1 CONT The future William I of England (1028-1087)
1 CONT Adelaide of Normandy (1030-c. 1083), who was married three times:
1 CONT Enguerrand II, Count of Ponthieu
1 CONT Lambert II, Count of Lens
1 CONT Odo II of Champagne
1 CONT
1 CONT Pilgramage and Death
1 CONT
1 CONT After making his illegitimate son William his heir, he set out on pilgrimage to Jerusalem. According to the Gesta Normannorum Ducum he travelled by way of Constantinople, reached Jerusalem, and died on the return journey at Nicaea
1 CONC  on 2 July 1035. Some sources attribute his death to poison and date it to 1 or 3 July. His son William, aged about eight, succeeded him.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the historian William of Malmesbury, around 1086 William sent a mission to Constantinople and Nicaea, charging it with bringing his father's body back to be buried in Normandy. Permission was granted, but, having trav
1 CONC elled as far as Apulia (Italy) on the return journey, the envoys learned that William himself had meanwhile died. They then decided to re-inter Robert's body in Italy.
1 CONT <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_the_Magnificent>
