0 @N6000000003949665209@ NOTE byname ROBERT The MAGNIFICENT, or The DEVIL, French ROBERT le MAGNIFIQUE, or le DIABLE (d. July 1035, Nicaea), duke of Normandy (1027-35), the younger son of Richard II of Normandy and the father, by his mistress Arlette, of Willi
1 CONC am the Conqueror of England. On the death of his father (1026/27), Robert contested the duchy with his elder brother Richard III, legally the heir, until the latter's opportune death a few years later. A strong ruler, Robert succe
1 CONC eded in exacting the obedience of his vassals. On the death of Robert II the Pious, king of France (1031), a crisis arose over the succession to the French throne. The Duke gave his support to Henry I against the party favouring h
1 CONC is younger brother; in reward for his services he demanded and received the Vexin Français, a territory not far north of Paris. A patron of the monastic reform movement, he died while returning from a pilgrimage to Jerusalem. [Enc
1 CONC yclopaedia Britannica CD '97]
0 @N6000000003300509174@ NOTE
1 CONT Robert I was not well liked.  He died of poison, perhaps by his own follo wers.
1 CONT Robert was the Duke of Normandy from 1027 to 1035. He was the second so n of Richard II 288 . He succeeded his brother Richard III, who was Duk e for only one year, a fact that may indicate that Richard III may have b een murdered
1 CONC . Robert was the father of William the Conqueror 220 . Willia m's mother was Herleva 233 who Robert did not marry. Robert faced problem s as soon as he succeeded his brother, facing conflicts with Robert of Ro uen and Bishop Hugh 
1 CONC of Bayeux, both churchmen who owned large amounts o f land. He defeated Hugh and came to a compromise with Robert.
1 CONT Robert was not slow in assisting both the French King Henry I, and the En glish monarchy in times of trouble. He gave refuge to Edward and Albert t wo brothers. Edward was to become 'The Confessor' 293 , and was to promis e the En
1 CONC glish throne to Robert's son William. In 1035, Robert traveled t o the Holy lands on pilgrimage but before he left, he made sure that Will iam would become Duke of Normandy if he didn't return. He needed to hav e this confirmed, a
1 CONC s William was illegitimate. As it was, Robert was kill ed returning from Jerusalem, and so the eight year old William became Duk e. William's first years were in a duchy transformed by civil war after t he death of Robert.
1 CONT In the year 1033  Robert Assists English and French Kings
1 CONT Robert I, Duke of Normandy 232 was an ally of the French King Henry I , and also assisted two English brothers, Edward (to become Edward the Co nfessor) 293 and Alfred, sons of Ethelred, King of the English 43 , who w as overthrow
1 CONC n by Canute 320 in 1016. Robert may had tried to assist Edwar d and Albert in their attempts to retake the English throne back from Can ute. It may have been for this assistance that Edward was to promise Robe rt's son William (th
1 CONC e Conqueror) 220 the future crown of England.
1 CONT Though Robert never married Herleve according to Norman law or custom, h e was faithful to her and she to him until his death. He was called "Robe rt the Devil". Tab. Souv. Gen., France 22, Tab. 32, 48; Dukes of Normandy , France 
1 CONC 5,p 115-27; Keiser und Koenig Hist., Gen. Hist. 25,pt 1, p 100-0 1; Burke's Peerage, Eng., P, 1949, pref. p 252. !Ridipath's Histories; Bu rk's Landed Gentry; Gareth Rice; !The Noble Lineage of the Delaware Wes t Family by Anne Fo
1 CONC x 1958; 6th Duke of Normandy, murdered his brother Ric hard abducted Herleva of Falaise dau of the village tanner, and kept he r a virtual prisoner at the Castle of Falaise, where in due time she bor e him a son, William the Conqu
1 CONC eror.
1 CONT Archbishop of Rouen
1 CONT Robert went on a pilgrimage to Jerusalem in the Holy Land to atone for hi s sins
1 CONT and while returning from Asia Minor, he came down with the "quarton fever " and
1 CONT died in Asia Minor.He is believed to be the source of the ledgend that hi s
1 CONT mother sold his soul to the devil and he tried to atone with great acts o f
1 CONT penance in later life.
0 @N6000000003051310072@ NOTE Robert contributed to the restoration of Henry King of France to his throne, and received from the gratitude of that monarch, the Vexin, as an additional to his patrimonial domains.
1 CONT In the 8th year of his reign, curiosity or devotion induced him to undertake a pilgrimage to the Holy Land, where the fatigues of the journey and the heat of the climate so impaired his consitution he died on his way home.
1 CONT Some sources call him Robert I the Magnificent
0 @N6000000002187570240@ NOTE Robert the Magnificent
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert I, called "The Magnificent" (French, "le Magnifique") for his love of finery, and also called "The Devil" was the son of Duke Richard II of Normandy and Judith, daughter of Conan I, Duke of Brittany.
1 CONT
1 CONT When his father died, his elder brother Richard succeeded, whilst he became Count of Hiémois. When Richard died a year later, there were great suspicions that Robert had Richard murdered, hence his other nickname, "Robert le diabl
1 CONC e" (the devil). He is often mis-identified with the legendary Robert the Devil. Robert aided King Henry I of France against Henry's rebellious brother and mother, and for his help he was given the territory of the Vexin. He also i
1 CONC ntervened in the affairs of Flanders, supported Edward the Confessor, who was then in exile at Robert's court, and sponsored monastic reform in Normandy.
1 CONT
1 CONT By his mistress, Herleva, he was father of two children:
1 CONT
1 CONT the future William the Conqueror. 
1 CONT Adeliza, Countess of Aumale, married three times 
1 CONT Enguerrand II of Ponthieu 
1 CONT Lambert II, Count of Lens 
1 CONT Eudes II of Champagne. 
1 CONT After making his illegitimate son William his heir, he made a pilgrimage to Jerusalem and died at Nicaea between 1-3 of July, 1035. His son William, aged about eight, succeeded him
0 @N6000000000943486289@ NOTE [Norvell.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT [Eno.ftw]
1 CONT
1 CONT BIOGRAPHY: Duke of Normandy 1028/ 1035
1 CONT
1 CONT BIOGRAPHY: Robert contributed to the restoration of Henry King of France to his throne, and received from the gratitude of that monarch, the Vexin, as an addition to his patrimonial domains.  In the 8th year of his reign, curiosit
1 CONC y or devotn induced him to undertake a pilgrimage to the Holy Land, where the fatiges of the journey and the heat of the climate so impaired his consitution he died on his way home.  Some sources call him Robert I the Magnificent.
0 @N6000000003219690911@ NOTE Robert, called "The Magnificent" (French, "le Magnifique") for his love of finery, and also called "The Devil" was the son of Duke Richard II of Normandy and Judith, daughter of Conan I, Duke of Brittany.
1 CONT
1 CONT When his father died, his elder brother Richard succeeded, whilst he became Count of Hiémois. When Richard died a year later, there were great suspicions that Robert had Richard murdered, hence his other nickname, "Robert le diabl
1 CONC e" (the devil). He is sometimes identified with the legendary Robert the Devil. Robert aided King Henry I of France against Henry's rebellious brother and mother, and for his help he was given the territory of the Vexin. He also i
1 CONC ntervened in the affairs of Flanders, supported Edward the Confessor, who was then in exile at Robert's court, and sponsored monastic reform in Normandy.
1 CONT
1 CONT By his mistress, Herleva of Falaise, he was father of two children:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. the future William the Conqueror (1028-1087)
1 CONT    2. Adelaide of Normandy (1030-c. 1083), who was married three times:
1 CONT          1. Enguerrand II, Count of Ponthieu
1 CONT          2. Lambert II, Count of Lens
1 CONT          3. Odo II of Champagne
1 CONT
1 CONT After making his illegitimate son William his heir, he set out on pilgrimage to Jerusalem. According to the Gesta Normannorum Ducum he travelled by way of Constantinople, reached Jerusalem, and died on the return journey at Nicaea
1 CONC  on 2 July 1035. Some sources attribute his death to poison and date it to 1 or 3 July. His son William, aged about eight, succeeded him.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the historian William of Malmesbury, around 1086 William sent a mission to Constantinople and Nicaea, charging it with bringing his father's body back to be buried in Normandy. Permission was granted, but, having trav
1 CONC elled as far as Apulia (Italy) on the return journey, the envoys learned that William himself had meanwhile died. They then decided to re-inter Robert's body in Italy.
0 @N6000000006236142936@ NOTE Robert contributed to the restoration of Henry King of France tohis throne, and received from the gratitude of that monarch, the Vexin, asan additional to his patrimonial domains. In the 8th year of his reign,curiosity or devotion
1 CONC  induced him to undertake a pilgrimage to the Holy Land, where the fatigues of the journey and the heat of the climate so impaired his consitution he died on his way home.
