0 @N6000000005076736901@ NOTE Saint Edward 'the Confessor', King of England was born between 1003 and 1005 at Islip, Oxfordshire, England.1 He was the son of Æthelred II 'the Unready', King of England and Emma de Normandie.2 He married Eadgyth of Wessex, daugh
1 CONC ter of Godwine, Earl of Wessex and Gytha (?), on 23 January 1045.1 He died on 5 January 1066 at Westminster Palace, Westminster, London, England.1 He was buried on 6 January 1066 at Westminster Abbey, Westminster, London, England.
1 CONC 1
1 CONT      Saint Edward 'the Confessor', King of England succeeded to the title of King Edward of England on 8 June 1042.1 He was crowned King of England on 3 April 1043 at Winchester Cathedral, Winchester, Hampshire, England.1
1 CONT      Hardicanute's death ended the male line of the Danish royal house and Edward,the son of Ethelred II, was elected King at London before Hardicanute had been buried. He was crowned at Winchester on Easter Day 1043. Edward was a
1 CONC n ascetic and had been brought up in Normandy after Canute came to the throne. He neglected the defence of the land, devoting himself largely to religious matters and founded Westminster Abbey in 1052. Edward made several Normans 
1 CONC English bishops and it is alleged that in 1051 he promised William, Duke of Normandy, that he should succeed him as King of England. He laid the foundations of the Cinque Ports. He married the daughter of Earl Godwin, the Danish E
1 CONC arl of Wessex, and there was much intrigue between the Anglo-Danish and Norman elements at the English court. Edward left England weak politically and militarily. He died on Thursday 5th January 1066 and was canonised in 1161. St 
1 CONC Edward the Confessor was the country's patron saint until St George replaced him a few centuries later. On 7 February 1161 , he was canonised.3
1 CONT Citations
1 CONT [S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Family: A Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 32. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Family.
1 CONT [S58] E. B. Fryde, D. E. Greenway, S. Porter and I. Roy, editors, Handbook of British Chronology, 3rd edition (London, U.K.: Royal Historical Society, 1986), page 29. Hereinafter cited as Handbook of British Chronology.
1 CONT [S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Family, page 34.
0 @N6000000007329555529@ NOTE [Kopi av ROYALS.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Reign: 1042-66.  He founded Westminster Abbey and was canonized less than a
1 CONT century after his death by Pope Alexander III in 1161.    He was succeded by
1 CONT Harold II. Edward was crowned in Winchester Cathedral on Easter Sunday,
1 CONT 3 April 1043 with great ceremony.
0 @N6000000000945053342@ NOTE Hennes foreldre var rike, faren var Guttorm Jarl i Sverker-partiet, han eide gods i Östergötland, 
1 CONT Västergötland, Småland, på Gotland og i Danmark. Moren er ukjent.
1 CONT Helena ble i ung alder, antagelig 18 år gammel, gift med Esbern Snare. Deres ekteskap varte i
1 CONT 26 år, men de fikk kun et felles barn, Ingeborg av Kalundborg.
1 CONT Kort tid etter Esberns død i 1204 fikk hun sønnen Knud med kong Valdemar II Sejr av
1 CONT Danmark. Han ble senere hertug av Reval.
1 CONT Da Valdemar giftet seg, flyttet Helena og sønnen Knud til Östergötland. Vår Frues Kapell med
1 CONT tilhørende prebende ble stiftet av Helena og Knud, tidligst i 1223.
1 CONT Helena ble begravet i den daværende domkirken i Linköping, tidligst på 1230-tallet.
1 CONT Etter morens død oppførte hennes barn, Ingeborg og Knud, Helena-kapellet i nåværende
1 CONT Tisvildeleje.
0 @N6000000004533031042@ NOTE <b>Helene,</b> -1204-, Esbern Snares Hustru, var Datter af den ansetesvenske Jarl Guttorm, som havde hentet Kong Carl Sverkersens Brud iDanmark og ved samme Lejlighed stod Fadder til Valdemar I's s Søn Knud(1163). Rimeligvis har h
1 CONC un været opkaldt efter den hellige Helene afSkøfde, en fejret Helgeninde, der blev kanoniseret 1164 og er velkjendt i nordsjællandske Folkesagn. Da Esbern Snare havde mistet sinanden Hustru, Ingeborg, ægtede han, skjønt til Aars, 
1 CONC den unge svenskeJarledatter. I dette Ægteskab fødtes Ingeborg «af Kalundborg» ogmaaske flere andre Børn. Strax efter Esberns Død (1204) blev H. KongValdemar II's Frille og fødte ham Sønnen Knud. Men da Valdemar ægtedeDagmar (1205)
1 CONC , ophørte vistnok deres Forhold, og hun synes at haveforladt Danmark. I alt Fald blev hun ikke som Esberns 2 førsteHustruer jordfæstet i Sorø, og af Diplomer fra det 14. Aarhundredefremgaar det, at hun ved Linkøping Domkirke stift
1 CONC ede vor Frue Kapelmed tilhørende Præbende; der har hun vistnok valgt sit Gravsted, oghendes Gave blev senere forøget af hendes Sønnesøn Svantopolk Knudsenog hans Æt, Valdemar II udmærkede sin og H.s Søn
1 CONT med Hertugnavnet og skjænkede ham tillige sit Gods i Sverige, og H.har ogsaa efterladt ham en betydelig Arv her.
1 CONT
1 CONT Suhm, Hist. af Danmark IX, 192 ff.
1 CONT J. Steenstrup, Vore Folkeviser i Middelald. S. 248 ff.
1 CONT
1 CONT <i>Hans Olrik.</i>
0 @N6000000000944957493@ NOTE Levde fra 1190 til 1205. 
1 CONT Helene ble i ung alder gift med Esbern Snare som var en dansk stormann.
1 CONT Etter Esberns død i 1204 fikk hun sønnen Knud med kong Valdemar II Sejr av Danmark
0 @N6000000000437030971@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> II, Earl Of Orkney & Caithnes "The Mighty"
0 @N6000000002187914542@ NOTE Thorfinn
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Thorfinn Sigurdson (c. 1009 - 1065) is known to history as Thorfinn the Mighty, as at his height, he controlled as earl all of Orkney and Shetland, the Hebrides, Caithness and Sutherland.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was a boy of five when his father Sigurd Hlodvirsson was slain at the Battle of Clontarf near Dublin in 1014, leaving him in competition with his brothers (Sumarlidi, Einar and Brusi) for a share of the Norse earldom of Orkney.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thorfinn was brought up under the protection of his maternal grandfather Malcolm II of Scotland and was made Earl of Caithness and Sutherland on his father's death. When his brother Sumarlidi died (probably in 1019/1020), his shar
1 CONC e of the Earldom was taken by Einar, but he held it for only a short time before being killed in 1020 by Thorkell [either Sumarlidason or Amundison - Orkneyinga Saga is unclear on this identification], known as Fostri because he h
1 CONC ad been the foster father of Thorfinn when he was younger. A third of the earldom was then held by Brusi, with the other two thirds in dispute, and so both Thorfinn and Brusi sailed for Norway to petition the King for justice in t
1 CONC heir claims.
1 CONT
1 CONT The earldom was split in three, with Thorfinn and Brusi each holding a third, and the King holding the remainder, over which he appointed Brusi as his steward. With the fall of Norway to Canute, Thorfinn bargained his way to contr
1 CONC ol over two thirds of the earldom, and full control of its defences. On Brusi's death, perhaps around 1035, he gained full control of the Earldom.
1 CONT
1 CONT Around 1040, Duncan I of Scotland tried to replace Thorfinn as Earl of Caithness, which led to two battles between the men of Orkney and the Scots - a sea battle at Deerness in Orkney, and on land at Tarbat Ness, and in both cases
1 CONC  Thorfinn was victorious despite being outnumbered.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thorfinn ruled alone in Orkney until the arrival of his nephew Rognvald Brusason, who claimed his father's third of the Earldom, plus the third owed to Magnus I of Norway, claiming that King Magnus had granted him the stewardship 
1 CONC of his lands. The earldom was again split, but in time the earls fell out and engaged in a battle at sea against each other, after which Rognvald tried to have Thorfinn burned to death while he was staying at a farm. Surviving the
1 CONC  attempt, Thorfinn gathered his supporters and set the same trap for Rognvald, and although he escaped from the fire, he was tracked down and killed by Thorkel Fostri.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thorfinn was now secure in his possession of the Earldom, so much so that when he visited King Harald III of Norway after the death of Magnus, he extended his trip into a pilgrimage to Rome.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thorfinn is thought to have died around 1065, although the Orkneyinga Saga suggests that the held the rank of Earl for 75 years. He was married to Ingibiorg, daughter of Finn Arnesson, an allied and in-law of Olav II of Norway and
1 CONC  Harald II of Norway. Ingibiorg (or possibly a daughter by the same name) was later married to Malcolm III of Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thorfinn was succeeded by his two sons, Paul and Erlend, who ruled jointly. Erlend's son is Saint Magnus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thorfinn was buried in the grounds of St. Magnus Church, Birsay, Mainland Orkney. He is known to history as Thorfinn the Mighty, and at his height, he controlled all of Orkney and Shetland, the Hebrides, Caithness and Sutherland, 
1 CONC and his influence extended over much of the north of Scotland. The Orkneyinga Saga makes a grander claim - that he controlled seven earldoms in Scotland. As there were only seven earldoms in total, this seems to claim he was King 
1 CONC of Scots, but of course the king at this time is recorded as being Macbeth - some have theorised that they are the same person, since Macbeth seems to spring from nowhere, and the two are recorded as going on pilgrimage to Rome in
1 CONC  the same year, but Macbeth is recorded as dying in 1057, leaving this idea as only a slim possibility.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT See also
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Macbeth, King of Scotland
1 CONT     * Orkneyinga Saga
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
