0 @N6000000006348204565@ NOTE Malcolm III (1058-93)
1 CONT Malcolm Canmore ('great head' or 'chief') was the eldest son of Duncan I. After his father's death, he found refuge in England with his uncle Siward of Northumbria, where he stayed for more than 14 years. His first wife was Ingibj
1 CONC org, widow of Earl Thorfinn of Orkney. She died, and in about 1070 he married Margaret, great-niece of King Edward the Confessor of England. She had sought refuge in Scotland with her brother, Edgar the Atheling (Anglo-Saxon heir 
1 CONC to the English throne), when William I excluded him from the English succession. Margaret had a strong influence over her husband, who revered her piety and secretly had jewel-encrusted bindings made for her religious books, which
1 CONC  he himself was unable to read, never having learned to do so. He also substituted Saxon for Gaelic as the court language. According to Margaret's biographer, she corresponded with Lanfranc, Archbishop of Canterbury, brought Bened
1 CONC ictine monks to Dunfermline and did away with local usages in the Scottish Church. Margaret also began building what was later to be known as St Margaret's Chapel, situated on the highest part of Edinburgh Castle.
1 CONT Malcolm was determined to extend his kingdom southwards and take advantage of the upheaval caused by the Norman Conquest. Making the excuse that he was supporting the claim to the English throne of his brother-in-law Edgar Athelin
1 CONC g, Malcolm invaded England five times (he was a formidable warrior-king, having killed his two predecessor kings). Three times defeated, Malcolm was forced under the treaty of Abernethy in 1072 to become 'the man' of the English k
1 CONC ing and give up his son Duncan as a hostage. Malcolm and his eldest son were finally killed in battle at Alnwick, Northumberland on 13 November 1093, aged about 62. His wife died when they brought her the news at Edinburgh Castle.
1 CONC  She was canonised in 1249.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Malcolm's death, the frontier between the kingdoms of Scotland and England was clearly defined for the first time. Anglo-Norman influence in Scotland was promoted by the subsequent marriages of Malcolm's sons to English brid
1 CONC es.
1 CONT
1 CONT Source:  Official Website of the British Government
0 @N6000000003232733372@ NOTE Found Refuge In England With Uncle Siward Of Norhumbria When Father Died. Never Learned To Read.
1 CONT Substituted Saxon For Gaelic As The Court Language.
1 CONT
1 CONT Alias:<ALIA> Canmore/Ceanmohr or big/great head or chief
1 CONT
1 CONT Some sources list his death in 1098.
1 CONT Slayed MACBETH (see Shakespeare).
1 CONT According to SCOTTISH ORIGINS by Robert M. Gunn
1 CONT (http://www.clan.com/history/features/origins/4.html):
1 CONT "Malcolm III grew up in exile in England and spoke English as his primary tongue. He also visited Normandy, made friends there, and was also under their influence...He married Margaret, an English Princess who had taken refuge in 
1 CONC Scotland along with her brother Edgar the Æþeling, after the Norman conquest of England begun in 1066." Mael Coluim Ceann Mora (Canmore-the Great Ruler), was King of Scots, 1058-1093. He killed mac Beth in 1057. He gave refuge to 
1 CONC Eadgar Eadweardsson in 1066. In 1072 he paid homage to Willelm le Bastard, but made five raids into northern England over the next few years. He was killed on the last of these raids in 1093.
1 CONT
1 CONT Among his contributions was the establishment of primogeniture, succession by the eldest son. Prior kings were chosen by tanistry, the Celtic custom of choosing the ruler by election from the male heads of septs, or from all the m
1 CONC ales of a clan. This change greatly stabilized the political atmosphere by clearly identifying the successor.
1 CONT
1 CONT [Sargent.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT EDWARD THE CONFESSOR  U. OF CAL. PRESS
1 CONT
1 CONT Alias:<ALIA> /Longneck/
1 CONT Died at siege of Alnwick Castle [91502.ftw]
1 CONT
1 CONT [Direct Linage1.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Ruled 1057-1093
1 CONT Note:
1 CONT Malcolm III CANMORE (b. c. 1031--d. Nov. 13, 1093, near Alnwick, Norþhumberland, England.), king of Scotland from 1058 to 1093, founder of the dynasty that consolidated royal power in the Scottish kingdom.
1 CONT
1 CONT The son of King Duncan I (reigned 1034-40), Malcolm lived in exile in England during part of the reign of his father's murderer, Macbeth (reigned 1040-57). Malcolm killed Macbeth in battle in 1057 and then ascended the throne. Aft
1 CONC er the conquest of England by Willelm le Bastard, in 1066, Malcolm gave refuge to the Anglo-Saxon prince Edgar the Æþeling and his sisters, one of whom, Margaret (later Saint Margaret), became his second wife.
1 CONT
1 CONT Malcolm acknowledged the overlordship of Willelm in 1072 but nevertheless soon violated his feudal obligations and made five raids into England. During the last of these invasions he was killed by the forces of King Willelm II RVF
1 CONC VS (reigned 1087-1100). Except for a brief interval after Malcolm's death, the Scottish throne remained in his family until the death of Queen Margaret, the Maid of Norway, in 1290. Of Malcolm's six sons by Margaret, three succeed
1 CONC ed to the throne: Edgar (reigned 1097-1107), Alexander I (1107-24), and David I (1124-53). [Encyclopædia Britannica CD '97]
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Brian Tompsett, "interred at Escorial, Madrid, Spain."
1 CONT
1 CONT Malcolm III built Kindrochit Castle, Braemar, Aberdeenshire between 1057-1093.
1 CONT Note:
1 CONT What's with these death dates? Malcolm, his wife and two sons all died within 3 days of one another. Was there an overthrow? A plague? Interesting. Oops, just looked it up: Malcolm was assassinated in 1093 (apparently along with h
1 CONC is wife and two sons), replaced by Donaldbane, who was deposed by Duncan II, (assassinated) replaced by Donaldbane (again) who was again deposed, this time by King Edgar. Duncan I was assassinated by Macbeth (as described by Shake
1 CONC speare). All in all an astonishingly stormy period:
1 CONT
1 CONT Duncan I 1034-1040 assassinated
1 CONT Macbeth 1040-1057 assassinated
1 CONT Lulach 1057-1058 died
1 CONT Malcolm III 1058-1093 assassinated
1 CONT Donaldbane 1093-1094 deposed
1 CONT Duncan II May-Nov 1094 assassinated
1 CONT Donaldbane (again) 1094-1097 deposed
1 CONT Edgar 1097-1107 died
1 CONT Alexander I 1107-1124 [son of Malcolm III]
1 CONT David (Saint) 1124-1153 d. [son of Malcolm III]
1 CONT Malcolm IV 1153-1165 d. [son of Malcolm III]
1 CONT
1 CONT Begs the question--why was their daughter Margaret left unscathed? 7 years later, she married Henri Beauclerc the king of England. Why Alexander, David (Saint) and Edgar, all left alive, who went on to become kings of Scotland in 
1 CONC their own right?
1 CONT [Claypoole--Dic Davis Royal Line to Add.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT Malcolm III Canmore, King Of Scotland was crowned at Scone, 17 March
1 CONT 1057/8 was slain while besieging Alnwick Castle.
1 CONT Buried Holy Trinity Church, Dunfermline, Fifeshire, Scotland
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: 'Ancestrial Roots of Sixty Colonists Who Came to New England between 1623 and 1650', 1969, Frederick Lewis Weis, p 111. 'Royalty for
1 CONT Commoners', Roderick W. Stuart, 1993, p 2.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to records he Killed MacBeth [JamesLinage.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT [1748129.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT Ruled 1057-1093[JamesLinage.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT [1748129.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT Ruled 1057-1093
