0 @N6000000002187837154@ NOTE Roman the Great
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Roman the Great or Roman Mstislavich (c. 1150-1205) was the Prince of Novgorod, Volhynia, and Halych.
1 CONT
1 CONT Roman was the son of Mstislav II of Kiev. During 1168-1170 he reigned in the Great Novgorod. From 1170 to 1199 he ruled in Volodymyr-Volynsky, which he inherited from his father. In 1187, he made an unsuccessful attempt to wrestle
1 CONC  from the boyars the principality of Halych. In 1197 he married Anna, by whom he had four children, including Danylo of Halych.
1 CONT
1 CONT Roman Mstislavich gained nation-wide fame by his victorious campaign against Polovtsi in 1197-1198, 1201, and 1204. In 1199, he became Prince of Halych, uniting Halichina and Volhynia lands in the mighty principality of Halych-Vol
1 CONC hynia. In 1202, he also captured Kiev, uniting all South-west Ruthenia under his rule. Prominent as a warlord, Roman the Great was also generally considered a wise politician. He lived mostly in peace with Hungary and Poland, but 
1 CONC had uneasy relationships with several other neighbouring states. He perished near the Polish city Zawichost during a march against Prince Leszek the White of Lesser Poland.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ingvar I Grand Prince of Kiev Succeeded by:
1 CONT Rostislav II 
0 @N6000000002187825310@ NOTE Mieszko III the Old
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT  Mieszko III the Old (Polish: Mieszko III Stary; 1121? – March 13, 1202), of the Piast Dynasty, was Duke of Greater Poland, 1138-1202, and High Duke of all Poland, with interruptions, 1173-1202.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mieszko was the son of Boleslaw III Wrymouth, Duke of Poland, and Salome of Berg. Mieszko was Boleslaw's third son, and the ducal couple's second.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mieszko was Duke of Greater Poland, 1138-1202, and Duke of Kraków and High Duke of all Poland, 1173-1177, 1190, 1198/1199, 1202.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mieszko married:
1 CONT
1 CONT ca. 1140, Elizabeth of Hungary, daughter of Prince Álmos, brother of the King of Hungary; 
1 CONT ca. 1154, Eudoksia of Kiev, daughter of Izjaslav II, Grand Duke of Kyiv (Kievan Rus'). 
1 CONT Mieszko's children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Odon I of Greater Poland; 
1 CONT Boleslaw of Kujawy; 
1 CONT Mieszko of Kalisz; and 
1 CONT Wladyslaw III Spindleshanks. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Biography
1 CONT In accordance with the testament of Poland's Duke Boleslaw III Wrymouth, Poland was divided into 4-5 hereditary provinces distributed among his sons, with the royal province of Kraków being reserved for the eldest son, who was to 
1 CONC be High Duke of all Poland.
1 CONT
1 CONT One of the provinces, Greater Poland, was granted to Boleslaw's third-oldest son, Mieszko (and was subsequenly divided among Mieszko's descendants and successors). In 1173 Mieszko was promoted to the throne of Kraków and became Hi
1 CONC gh Duke of Poland (Latin: dux maximus, dux totius Poloniae); but he was soon expelled from Kraków by his younger brother, Kazimierz II the Just, and from Greater Poland by his son. Mieszko III never abdicated his rights or ambitio
1 CONC ns, and returned to Kraków four times before he died in 1202.
0 @N6000000002187829077@ NOTE Wladyslaw III Spindleshanks
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Wladyslaw III Spindleshanks (Polish: Wladyslaw III Laskonogi; 1165? – 1231) was Duke of the province of Greater Poland and High Duke of all Poland, overlord of other Dukes, 1202-1206 and 1227-1228, during which periods he also was
1 CONC  Duke of Cracow, the duchy customarily held by the overlord.
1 CONT
1 CONT Wladyslaw became overlord of Poland in place of his cousin Leszek the White who was deposed. Wladyslaw then was himself deposed in favor of Leszek from the overlordship. Only after twenty years, and a couple of reigns in between, 
1 CONC he succeeded to get restored.
1 CONT
1 CONT Wladyslaw was the son of Mieszko III the Old and his successor in their family part-realm of Greater Poland.
