0 @N6000000000944948318@ NOTE Dronning. Født ca. 1141. Død 05.05.1198. 
1 CONT Datidens Gårdarike er nåværende Russland. Sentrum besto av de to byene Holmgard (Novgorod) og Kønugard (Kiev).
1 CONT Da Valdemar I. og Knud Magnussen hadde nærmet seg hverandre, ble det i 1154 avtalt et ekteskap mellom Valdemar og Knuds halvsøster, Sophia. Hun var også i slekt med Valdemar, idet Valdemars mor, Ingeborg, var søster til Sophiesfar
1 CONC far Fyrst Vesvolodod av Novgorod. (Amn.: Dette er feil, hun var hans niese.) Da Sophie ikke selv eide gods i Danmark, lovet Knud å utstyre henne med en tredjedel av sin egen farsarv. Ennå før Valdemars endelige seier på Gratchede 
1 CONC ble deres bryllup feiret i Viborg i 1157.
1 CONT Sophia ble annen gang gift ca. 1184 med Ludvig III, landgreve av Thüringen, som senere forstøtte henne.
1 CONT I ?Die Nachkommen Karls des Grossen? av Erich Brandenburg angis som hennes far Wolodar, fyrste av Minsk i 1150 og av Gorcdez i 1158, død etter 1167. Dette kan ifølge Mogens Bugge ikke være riktig. Han anser, som også danske kilder
1 CONC , at hennes far var Vladimir av Novgorod (av Halicz hos Bugge).
1 CONT Det er imidlertid enighet om at hennes mor var Richiza av Polen.
0 @N6000000000945049202@ NOTE Berengaria var sin fars yngste datter. Da moren døde i 1198 etter 17 års ekteskap, og 
1 CONT etter å ha brakt 9 barn til verden, er hun rimeligvis født ca. 1192. Etter morens død ble hun
1 CONT oppdratt av sin eldste søster som hadde vært gift med kong Alfons av Leon. De portugisiske
1 CONT krøniker antar at hun døde ugift, men muligens har hun under søsterens stridigheter med deres
1 CONT eldre bror kong Alfons II av Portugal begitt seg til sin yngre bror Ferdinand. Han hadde ved
1 CONT ekteskap blitt greve i Flandern, hvorfra hun lett kunne bli kjent i Danmark.
1 CONT Berengaria kom utvilsomt til Danmark fra Flandern. Hennes bryllup med kong Valdemar
1 CONT omtales av en nederlandsk kilde, Valdemars årbok kaller henne uttrykkelig ved samme
1 CONT anledning ?Grev Ferrand of Flanderns Søster?. Ekteskapet hadde vistnok politiske motiv. Den
1 CONT portugisiske kongesønn Ferrand ble ved Philip Augusts innflydelse i 1212 grev av Flandern,
1 CONT men viste seg snart som en urolig vasal. Han hadde nektet sin lensherre hærfølge og sluttet
1 CONT forbund med Frankrikes fiender, Johan av England og Otto IV av Tyskland. At Berengaria ble
1 CONT ektet med Valdemar, synes å være et drag i disse års diplomatiske spill, et forsøk til å binde
1 CONT Danmarks konge til alliansen mot Philip August. Ved bryllupet skjenket kong Valdemar etter
1 CONT samråd med rikets stormenn Berengaria en morgengave hvis slag og omfang er ukjent, men
1 CONT gavens betydning fremgår ved den merkelige omstendighet, at man søkte pavelig stadfestelse.
1 CONT Såvel Innocens III som Honorius III har bekreftet den.
1 CONT Berengaria (portugisisk: Berenguela) fødte sin mann sønnene Erik, Abel og Christoffer,
1 CONT og datteren Sophie. Hennes ekteskap faller sammen med Valdemars største politiske
1 CONT maktutfoldelse, men hun nevnes aldri ved hans side. Kun en gang forekommer hun som vitne
1 CONT ved et brev utstedt i kongsgården på Samsø i 1216, og ingenting fremgår som forteller om
1 CONT hennes personlighet og innflytelse. Overfallet på Lyø, kongens fangenskap og rikets
1 CONT sammenbrudd opplevde hun ikke, men i løsesummen ved Valdemars frigivelse inngikk alt det
1 CONT guld som hadde tilhørt dronningens pryd. Unntatt er kun kronen og det hun før sin død hadde
1 CONT gitt til kirker og klostre.
1 CONT Hun etterlot seg et rykte om stor skjønnhet, men også om hovmod og strenghet.  Dette
1 CONT har fabeldiktningen benyttet til å gi henne navnet ?Bengjærd?. Da man i 1855  åpnet
1 CONT kongegravene i Ringsted, fant man i Berengarias grav rester av en kvinne av statelig høyde,
1 CONT vakker legemsbygning og fine trekk. Men den ufred som var tilføyet graven, viser også hatet
1 CONT mot henne. Det bør bemerkes at ingen av hennes sønner oppkalte noen av sine døtre etter
1 CONT henne.
0 @N6000000000945049231@ NOTE Sophie var datter til den polske hertugdatteren Richiza (Richeza, Ryksa, Rixa) 
1 CONT Boleslawsdatter i hennes annet ekteskap med fyrsten Vladimir (Valedar, Volodar). Det er
1 CONT omstritt hvem denne Vladimir var, men i sin stamtavle over de danske konger kaller Abbed
1 CONT Vilhelm [ca. 1125-1203] Sophies far ?Valedar, russernes konge?.
1 CONT I sitt første ekteskap med Magnus, sønn til kong Niels I Svendsson den Gamle av Danmark,
1 CONT fikk Richiza sønnen Knud, senere Knud Magnusson III av Danmark. Knud lot seg hylde som
1 CONT konge på Jylland og Fyn, mens Svend III Grathe var hans medkonge på Sjælland. Stridigheter
1 CONT mellom de to kulminerte ved blodgildet i Roskilde 1157 hvor Knud ble myrdet. Richiza giftet
1 CONT seg en tredje gang med kong Sverker I den Eldre av Sverige, og fikk med ham sønnen
1 CONT Bulislaw (Boleslaw).
1 CONT Knud og Sophie var altså halvsøsken.
1 CONT Da Valdemar og Knud III Magnusson hadde nærmet seg hverandre, ble det i 1154 avtalt
1 CONT ekteskap mellom Sophie og Valdemar. Da Sophie selv ikke eide gods i Danmark, lovet Knud
1 CONT å utstyre henne med en tredjedel av sin egen farsarv. Allerede før Valdemars endelige seier på
1 CONT Grathehede ble deres bryllup feiret i Viborg i 1157.
1 CONT Svend Aggesen [ca. 1130 - ca. 1185] berømmer Sophie for hennes store skjønnhet.
1 CONT Valdemar og Sophie hadde følgende barn:
1 CONT Sophie, gift med greve Siegfried III av Orlamünde, død ca. 1210.
1 CONT 1163: Knud IV av Danmark, gift med Gertrude av Bayern, død 1202.
1 CONT 1170: Valdemar II Sejr av Danmark, gift med Berengaria av Portugal, død 1241.
1 CONT Richiza, gift med kong Erik Knutsson av Sverige, død 1220.
1 CONT Ingeborg, gift med kong Filip II August av Frankrike, død 1236.
1 CONT Helene, gift med hertug Wilhelm av Lüneburg, død 1233.
1 CONT Dertil to døtre som gikk i kloster og som vi ikke sikkert kjenner navnene til.
1 CONT En av disse sistnevnte døtre ble forlovet med en sønn til keiser Frederik Barbarossa på et
1 CONT tidspunkt da Valdemar søkte samarbeide med keiseren. Etter Valdemars død fortsatte Knud IV
1 CONT denne politikk, idet han lot sin mor gifte seg med keiserens betrodde mann, greve Ludvig III
1 CONT av Thüringen. Senere, da forholdet mellom konge og keiser ble mer spent, ble såvel Sophie
1 CONT som hennes datter forstøtt av sine ektemenn og sendt tilbake til Danmark. Sophie skal ha blitt
1 CONT meget bitter av denne behandling ifølge Arnold av Lübeck som skrev ?Chronica Slavorum?
1 CONT 1172-1209 og døde i 1211 eller 1214. De danske kilder omtaler ikke denne pinlige episode.
1 CONT Sophie døde 05.05.1198 og ble begravet i Ringsted.
0 @N6000000002187829287@ NOTE Polatsk (Belarusian: ??´?????, ??´????, also spelt as Polacak, Polish: Polock, Russian: ??´????, also transliterated as Polotsk, Polotzk, Polock) is the most historic city in Belarus, situated on the Dvina river. It is the center 
1 CONC of Polatsk district in Viciebsk voblast. The population is approximately 79,000 residents.
0 @N6000000006127688390@ NOTE Sofia av Minsk drottning av Danmark. Född cirka 1140, död 5 maj 1198. Gift 23 oktober 1157 med kung Valdemar I Knutsson av Danmark.Dotter till polska prinsessan Rikissa av Polen i hennes andra gifte - efter sin förste makes, dansk
1 CONC e prinsen Magnus Nilssons, död - med en rysk furste, antingen Volodar av Minsk eller Vladimir av Halicz, trolovades 1154 med och äktade, eftermordet på hennes halvbroder kung Knut Magnusson, 1157 Valdemar I. Hon var mycket vacker,
1 CONC  men skildras i folkvisorna som elak och hämndgirig.Enligt dessa lät hon innebränna Valdemars älskarinna Tove i en badstuga och vållade hans syster Liten Kirstens olycka, men ingenting härav kan historiskt bevisas. Efter att ha bl
1 CONC ivit änka 1182 gifte sig Sofia med lantgreven Ludvig av Thüringen1186, men han skickade henne snart hånfullt tillbaka till Danmark, där hon avled.
0 @N6000000004533048427@ NOTE Sophie, Grevinde af Orlamiinde, -1181-, ældste Datter af
1 CONT Kong Valdemar og Dronning Sophie; hun blev i Slesvig bortgiftet
1 CONT til Grev Sigfred af Orlamiinde i Thiiringen (1181). Han sendtes
1 CONT et Par Aar senere af Kejser Frederik til Kong Knud med
1 CONT P'or-langende om, at denne skulde hylde Kejseren som Lensherre, men
1 CONT blev djærvt afvist. Om S.s Skæbne vides i øvrigt intet; hun fødte
1 CONT sin Ægtefælle 7 Børn, af hvilke Albert, hvis Historie tilhører
1 CONT Danmark (I, 168), var den ældste. Sigfred døde 1206.
1 CONT
1 CONT Johannes C. H. R. Steenstrup.
0 @N6000000002486699748@ NOTE Hos nogle historikere kaldes han den 6., idet disse regner Gorms mulige far, Hardeknud og Hardeknud, søn af Knud d. Store, med til Knud'erne. 
1 CONT Som 7 årig, i 1170, blev han på faderens ønske kronet som medkonge. Da han blev konge i 1182, forlangte den tyske kejser, Friedrich Barbarossa, at Knud skulle aflægge lensed. Knud afslog. Fortsat togter mod venderne. I 1185  er ve
1 CONC nderne
1 CONT tilsyneladende slået, kongen tager titlen "de Venders" (i brug til og med Fr. IX 1972). Udvider riget mod syd, når Elben 1202.
1 CONT 1175 Første kendte influenza-epidemi. Ordet "Jul" dukker op. 1201 Absalon dør i Roskilde.
0 @N6000000006127688397@ NOTE Knut VI, da. Knud VI, född 1162, död 12 november 1202, kung av Danmark från 1182. Han var äldste son till Valdemar den store och Sofia av Minsk och gifte sig med Gertrud av Sachsen. Äktenskapet blev barnlöst och han kom att eftert
1 CONC rädas av sin brorValdemar Sejr.Under Knut VI:s tid som kung flyttades den danska gränsen fram till Elbe och den skånelagen blev den första danska landskapslagen att bli nedtecknad.
0 @N6000000002187829316@ NOTE Valdemar II of Denmark
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT     "Valdemar the Victorious" redirects here. For the novel by Bernhard Severin Ingemann, see Valdemar the Victorious (novel).
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Valdemar II (May 9, 1170 or 28 June 1170—March 28, 1241, Vordingborg), called Valdemar the Conqueror or Valdemar the Victorious (Valdemar Sejr), was the King of Denmark from November 12, 1202 until his death in 1241. The nickname 
1 CONC Sejr is a later invention and was not used during the King's own lifetime.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Life
1 CONT     * 2 Family and children
1 CONT     * 3 In memoriam
1 CONT     * 4 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Life
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the second son of King Valdemar I and Sophia Valadarsdattir, a Varangian princess. When Valdemar's father died, young Valdemar was only twelve years old. He was named Duke of Southern Jutland but couldn't rule until he came
1 CONC  of age. Bishop Valdemar Knudsen of Schleswig was appointed regent for him. Bishop Valdemar was an ambitious man and disguised his own ambitions as young Valdemar's. When Bishop Valdemar was named Archbishop of Lund, his plot to o
1 CONC verthrow King Canute with the help of German nobility and sit on Denmark's throne himself was revealed.
1 CONT
1 CONT Duke Valdemar realized the threat Bishop Valdemar presented. He invited the archbishop to meet him in Åbenrå in 1192. When the archbishop arrived, Duke Valdemar arrested Bishop Valdemar and imprisoned him in Søborg Tower on Zealan
1 CONC d for the next 13 years. Young Valdemar faced another threat from Count Adolph of Rendsburg. Adolph tried to stir up other German counts to take southern Jutland from Denmark to assist Bishop Valdemar's plot to take the throne. Wi
1 CONC th the bishop in prison, Duke Valdemar went after Count Adolph and with his own troop levies march south and captured Adolph's new fortress at Rendsburg. He defeated and captured the count later that year and sent him to sit in a 
1 CONC cell next to Bishop Valdemar. Three years later Duke Valdemar let Count Adolph buy his way out of prison by ceding all of Schleswig north of the Elbe to Valdemar. Duke Valemar's elder brother, Canute VI died unexpedtedly at age 40
1 CONC  leaving no heirs. In 1202, Duke Valdemar was proclaimed king at the Jutland Assembly (Danish:landsting).
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1203 Valdemar invaded and conquered Lybeck and Holstein, adding them to the territories controlled by Denmark. In 1204 he attempted to influence the outcome of the Norwegian succession by leading a Danish fleet and army to Vike
1 CONC n, Norway in support of Erling Steinvegg the pretender to the Norwegian throne. This resulted in the second Bagler War which lasted until 1208, when the question of the Norwegian succession was temporarily settled. The Norwegian k
1 CONC ing owede allegiance to the king of Denmark.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1213 Valdemar instituted a "war" tax in Norway, and the peasants murdered Valdemar's tax collector at the Trøndelag Assembly and revolted. The uprising spread over several regions in Norway. Valdemar supported Emperor Frederick
1 CONC  II and was rewarded with the emperor acknowledging Denmark rule of Schleswig and Holstein, all of the Wendish lands and Pommerania. The Teutonic Knights who had been attempting to Christianize the peoples of the eastern Baltic, b
1 CONC ut by 1219 the were being hard pressed and turned to Valdemar for help. Pope Honorius III elevated Valdemar's invasion of Estonia into a crusade. Valdemar raised an army and a called all of Denmark's ship to gather to transport th
1 CONC e army eastward. Once assembled, the fleet numbered 1500 ships.
1 CONT
1 CONT When the army landed in Estonia, near modern-day Tallinn, the chiefs of the Estonians sat down with the Danes and exchanged gifts and agreed to acknowledge the Danish king as their overlord. A few of them allowed themselves to be 
1 CONC baptized which seemed to be a good sign. Just three days later On 15 June 1219 while the Danes were attending mass, thousands of Estonians broke into the Danish camp from all sides. Confusion reigned and things looked bad for Vald
1 CONC emar's crusade. Luckily for him, Vitslav of Rugen, gathered his men in a second camp and attacked the Estonians from the rear. During the Battle of Lyndanisse the legend says that whenever Bishop Sunesen raised his arms the Danes 
1 CONC surged forward and when his arms grew tired and he let them fall the Estonians turned the Danes back. Attendants rushed forward to raise his arms once again and the Danes surged forward again. At the heigt of the battle Bishop Sun
1 CONC sen prayed for a sign and it came in the form of a red cloth with a white cross which drifted down from the sky just as the Danes began to fall back. A voice was heard to say "When this banner is raised on high, you shall be victo
1 CONC rious!"[1] The Danes surged forward and won the battle. At the end of the day thousands of Estonians lay dead on the field, and Estonia was added to the Danish realm. Estonians were forcibly baptised as Christians. Valdemar ordere
1 CONC d the construction of a great fortress at Reval, near the site of the battle.[2] Eventually a city grew around the hilltop castle which is still called "the City of the Danes", Tallinn, in Estonian. The red banner with a white cro
1 CONC ss (Danish:Danebrog) has been the national flag of the Danes since 1219. It is Europe's oldest flag design still in modern use.
1 CONT
1 CONT Count Heinrich of Schwerin ingratiated himself into Valdemar's favor and in 1223 was invited to hunt with Valdemar on the island of Ly near Funen. The count'sship was brought to the place where the king and his men were camped. On
1 CONC ce the king and Prince Valdemar were bound and gagged and rushed aboard the count's ship. Heinrich raced back to Schwerin and threw King Valedmar and his son into Schwerin Tower. While Valdemar sat in prison for most of a year, mo
1 CONC st of the German territories tore themselves away from Denmark. Danish armies were defeated. To secure his release Valdemar had to acknowledge the break away territories in Germany, pay 44,000 silver marks, and sign a promise not 
1 CONC to seek revenge on Count Heinrich.
1 CONT
1 CONT Pope Honorius III excused Valdemar form his oath and immediately set about trying to restore the German territories. Valdemar concluded a treaty with his nephew Otto I, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg and headed south to take back what
1 CONC  he thought were his lands by right, but his luck deserted him. A series of Danish defeats culminating in the Battle of Børnehoved on 22 July 1227 cemented the lost German territories. Valdemar himself was saved only by the courag
1 CONC eous actions of a German knight who carried Valdemar to safety on his horse.
1 CONT
1 CONT From that time on Valdemar focused his efforts on domestic affairs. One of the changes he instituted was the feudal system where he gave properties to men with the understanding that they owed him service. This increased the power
1 CONC  of the noble families (Danish: hoj adelen) and gave rise to the lesser nobles (Danish:lavadelen) who controlled most of Denmark. The traditional free peasants lost the traditional rights and privileges they had enjoyed since Viki
1 CONC ng times.[3]
1 CONT
1 CONT Valdemar spent the remainder of his life putting together a code of laws for Jutland, Zealand and Skåne. These codes were used as Denmark's legal code until 1683. This was a significant change from the local law making that had be
1 CONC en made at the regional assemblies (Danish:landting). Several traditional methods of determing guilt or innocence were outlawed including trial by ordeal and trial by combat. The Code of Jutland (Danish:Jyske Lov) was approved at 
1 CONC meeting of the nobility at Vordingborg in 1241 just prior to Valdemar's death. Valdemar was buried next to Queen Damar at Ringsted.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family and children
1 CONT
1 CONT Before his first marriage Valdemar had been betrothed to Rixa of Bavaria, daughter of the Duke of Saxony. When that arrangement fell through, he married first Margarethe of Bohemia, also known as Queen Dagmar in 1205. She was the 
1 CONC daughter of Premysl Ottokar, King of Bohemia. She quickly won over the hearts of the Danes. By this marriage, Valdemar had a son, Valdemar, whom he elevated as co-king at Schleswig in 1218. Unfortunately, Prince Valdemar was accid
1 CONC entally shot while hunting at Refsnæs in 1231. Queen Margaret's birth year is not known for certain but was possibly 1189; she died in 1212/13. Old folk ballads says that on her death bed she begged Valdemar to marry Kirsten, the 
1 CONC daughter of Karl von Rise and not the "beautiful flower" Berengaria of Portugal (Danish:Bengerd). In other words she predicted Berengaria's sons' fight over the throne would bring trouble to Denmark.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Margaret's death, in order to build good relations with Flanders, (a commercially important territory to the west of Denmark's hostile southern neighbours), Valdemar married Berengária of Portugal in 1214. She was the orphan
1 CONC  daughter of King Sancho I of Portugal and a sister of Ferdinand, Count of Flanders where stayed until her marriage. Valdemar and Berengaria had three sons, Eric IV of Denmark, Abel of Denmark, and Christopher I of Denmark, and a 
1 CONC daughter, Sophie. She was beautiful, but so hard-hearted that she was generally hated by Danes until her early death in 1221.
1 CONT
1 CONT Valdemar's two queens play a prominent role in Danish ballads and myths - Dagmar as the soft, pious and popular ideal wife and Berengária (Bengjerd) as the beautiful and haughty woman.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Valdemar also had at least two illegitimate sons. Canute (Knud) whom he created as Duke of Reval (Estonia), Lolland, and Blekinge, born of a noblewoman, Helena Guttormsdotter, of Swedish birth and wife of an important Danish 
1 CONC nobleman. Nicolas (Danish:Niels) was created Count of Halland.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] In memoriam
1 CONT
1 CONT Because of his position as ”the king of Dannebrog” and as a legislator, Valdemar enjoys a central position in Danish history. To posterity the civil wars and dissolution that followed his death made him appear to be the last king 
1 CONC of a golden age.
1 CONT
1 CONT Since 1912, June 15 has officially been called Valdemarsdag (Valdemar's Day). The date now belongs to the group of 33 Danish annual Flag Days where Dannebrog is raised in celebration.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the upcoming film Arn: The Knight Templar he is played by Mads Mikkelsen.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT
